import{i as e}from"./jsx-runtime-D3jfb0Ew.js";var t=`the mistake you found in Spot the mistake`,n=[{why:`Spec 7.12.3: every port definition implicitly declares a nested conjugated port definition P::'~P'. The statement is true (see VERDICTS).`,from:`It is the conjugate of the same definition: its in and out directions are reversed.`,to:`Every port definition implicitly declares one nested conjugated port definition, here ChargingPort::'~ChargingPort', with its in and out directions reversed. You do not declare it yourself.`},{why:`Spec Table 4 has a redefinition line, and SysON lists New Redefinition. The real reason is that a compartment row has no creation handle (syson-week8-s2-pilot/REPORT.md).`,from:`Redefinition has no line to draw, so this is the one relationship made in the Explorer.`,to:`SysML v2 has a redefinition line, but padCount is a compartment row here and SysON gives a compartment row no handle to drag. So this is the one relationship made in the Explorer.`},{why:`Spec 8.3.22.4: ObjectiveMembership makes a requirement the objective. The diamond shows only ownership.`,from:`Nothing on it says objective: the diamond line from the use case is what makes it the objective, and the course figure shows it as a compartment instead.`,to:`No keyword on this diagram marks it as the objective; the word in its name is only SysON’s default name. The diamond line shows only that the use case owns the requirement. The model records that it is the objective, and the exported text uses the keyword objective. The course figure shows it as a compartment instead.`},{why:`Spec Table 26 and 8.2.3.27 define a «metadata» annotation node. Leaving it out is a course choice, not a tool matter.`,from:`The course draws no figure here: risk metadata is read in the text or in a tool’s element table, and how a tool shows metadata is tool-specific.`,to:`The course chose not to draw this section: risk metadata is read in the text or in a tool’s element table. The specification does define a graphical notation for it, the «metadata» annotation node.`},{why:`Spec 7.21: a requirement or concern has at most one subject (validateRequirementUsageOnlyOneSubject).`,from:`Every concern has exactly one subject, the thing it is about,`,to:`A concern has at most one subject, the thing it is about,`},{why:`Spec Table 23: a compartment is a notation choice for any subject, not a result of who performs the use case.`,from:`The enterprise performs it, so it sits inside the use case node as a compartment entry.`,to:`This drawing writes it in the subject compartment; SysML v2 also allows a «subject» node.`},{why:`Spec 7.25.2 (PDF 179): a use case usage must be defined by a single use case definition, so the two part definitions do not define the use case. They define the subject and the actor (listing learn_07_use_case lines 8 and 9), which 7.25.1 (PDF 177) models as part usages.`,from:`one objective, defined by two part definitions.`,to:`one objective. The subject and the actor are each defined by a part definition.`},{why:`Spec 7.6.1 (PDF 64): “A subsetting usage has a subset of the values of the subsetted usage.” KerML 7.3.4.4 (KerML PDF 61): the values of the subsetting feature are also values of the subsetted feature. “A kind of” is subclassification, also 7.6.1 (PDF 64). The section itself teaches subsetting as “one of”, not “a kind of”.`,from:`Subsetting would say that the grid is a kind of context.`,to:`Subsetting would say that the grid is one of the values of context.`},{why:`Spec 7.6.8 (PDF 76): a part definition that names no supertype implicitly subclassifies Parts::Part, so HubVertiport still inherits from the implicit library Part. What it lacks is padCount, a feature of Vertiport (7.6.1, PDF 64: a definition inherits the features of the definition it specializes).`,from:`but HubVertiport inherits nothing: no triangle joins it to Vertiport.`,to:`but HubVertiport does not inherit it: no triangle joins it to Vertiport.`},{why:`KerML 7.3.4.5 (KerML PDF 63): an owned redefinition must redefine a feature that would otherwise be inherited from a supertype of the owning type. As in the row above, padCount is not one here. Listing invalid_learn_redefine_not_inherited.sysml fails at line 14 with “Couldn't resolve reference to Feature 'padCount'.”`,from:`so it inherits nothing and its entry :>> padCount has nothing to redefine.`,to:`so it does not inherit padCount and its entry :>> padCount has nothing to redefine.`},{why:`Spec 7.5: private, protected and public are all import visibilities.`,from:`An import written without private or public.`,to:`An import written without a visibility (private, protected or public).`},{why:`Spec 7.27.4: a user-defined keyword names a metadata definition or a KerML metaclass.`,from:`A #keyword that names something other than a metadata definition.`,to:`A #keyword that names neither a metadata definition nor a KerML metaclass.`},{why:`Spec 7.27.4 (PDF 193): a user-defined keyword is the name or short name of a metadata definition (or KerML metaclass) preceded by #.`,from:`A user-defined keyword must name a metadata definition.`,to:`A user-defined keyword must name a metadata definition or a KerML metaclass.`},{why:`maxWaitP90 belongs to the requirement, not to the subject (listing learn_06_needs_requirements lines 24 and 25). Spec 7.21.2 (PDF 165) writes require constraint { massActual <= massRequired } over attributes declared in the requirement definition, and 7.21.1 (PDF 162) lets features of the subject be used as well. 7.20.1 (PDF 157): a constraint usage is a KerML Boolean expression.`,from:`It is a Boolean expression over features of the subject.`,to:`It is a Boolean expression over features of the subject and of the requirement.`},{why:`The same fact in the section note. The checked listing learn_06 reads require constraint { enterprise.passengerWaitP90 <= maxWaitP90 }, and maxWaitP90 is declared in the requirement.`,from:`A requirement adds a required constraint over a feature of the subject.`,to:`A requirement adds a required constraint over features of the subject and of the requirement.`},{why:`Spec 7.26.2 (PDF 184): an expose is declared like an import. 7.5.3 (PDF 60): the ::* suffix makes a namespace import, which takes the memberships of the namespace. 8.3.26.4 (PDF 419): a NamespaceExpose exposes those Memberships. So expose Stakeholders::* (listing learn_09_viewpoint_view line 21) exposes the members of the package.`,from:`The view exposes the Stakeholders package and asks for a tree rendering.`,to:`The view exposes the members of the Stakeholders package and asks for a tree rendering.`},{why:`Quoted as in the listing. A student who copies the unquoted line gets a syntax error.`,from:`A part usage is defined by a part definition (part enterprise : ENT-001).`,to:`A part usage is defined by a part definition (part enterprise : 'ENT-001').`},{why:`The listing does not read “part def ENT-001”, so this is written as prose.`,from:`“part def ENT-001” says that AeroLink exists in the context.`,to:`The part definition ENT-001 says that AeroLink exists in the context.`},{why:`Quoted as in the listing.`,from:`“part enterprise : ENT-001” is one usage`,to:`“part enterprise : 'ENT-001'” is one usage`},{why:`Quoted as in the listing.`,from:`stakeholder passengers : STK-001 creates`,to:`stakeholder passengers : 'STK-001' creates`},{why:`Quoted as in the listing.`,from:`from STK-011 to STK-002 reads`,to:`from 'STK-011' to 'STK-002' reads`},{why:`Quoted as in the listing.`,from:`frame CON-001 means NEED-001`,to:`frame 'CON-001' means NEED-001`},{why:`Quoted as in the listing.`,from:`satisfy REQ-STK-001 by aeroLink proves`,to:`satisfy 'REQ-STK-001' by aeroLink proves`},{why:`Also no longer says “must” beside part context, which has only the implicit Part.`,from:`A part usage must be defined by a part definition: the mistake in step 4.`,to:`A part usage is defined by a part definition, never by another usage: ${t}.`},{why:`Spec 7.6.1: “a part usage is defined by a part definition”, and “If no definition is specified explicitly, then the usage is defined implicitly by the most general definition”. So “must” does not sit beside part context, as in the row above.`,from:`A part usage must be defined by a part definition: here enterprise : 'ENT-001'.`,to:`A part usage is defined by a part definition, never by another usage: here enterprise : 'ENT-001'.`},{why:`As the row above.`,from:`a part usage must be defined by a part definition: the mistake from section 1.`,to:`a part usage is defined by a part definition, never by another usage: the mistake from section 1.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`so here you cannot make the mistake from step 4.`,to:`so here you cannot make ${t}.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`That is the mistake from step 4.`,to:`That is ${t}.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`Watch for the mistake from step 4 as well: SysON offers`,to:`Watch as well for ${t}: SysON offers`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`The wording differs from step 4, but the mistake is the same.`,to:`The wording differs from the message in Spot the mistake, but the mistake is the same.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`rejects a stakeholder defined by an attribute definition: the mistake in step 4.`,to:`rejects a stakeholder defined by an attribute definition: ${t}.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`not a second subject: this is the mistake from step 4.`,to:`not a second subject: this is ${t}.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`compare it with the SysML text in step 2 of this section.`,to:`compare it with the SysML text in Explore.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`It is the same model as the listing in step 2`,to:`It is the same model as the listing in Explore`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`Compare it with the diagram in step 2 of this section.`,to:`Compare it with the diagram in Explore.`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`the package of the checked listing in step 2,`,to:`the package of the checked listing in Explore,`},{why:`Named step.`,from:`Compare your diagram with the one in step 2 of this section.`,to:`Compare your diagram with the one in Explore.`},{why:`AeroLink is not available as a team enterprise, so the rows on this page are not the student’s own.`,from:`The Team Record: the register of IDs (ENT, STK, CON, NEED, REQ …) your team kept in Phase I.`,to:`The Team Record: the register of IDs (ENT, STK, CON, NEED, REQ …) each team keeps in Phase I. This one is AeroLink’s; yours has the same shape.`},{why:`As the row above.`,from:`ENT-001 is the enterprise row of your Phase I baseline; STK-001 and STK-004 are two rows of the Week 1 stakeholder register.`,to:`ENT-001 is the enterprise row of the AeroLink Phase I baseline; STK-001 and STK-004 are two rows of its Week 1 stakeholder register.`},{why:`The Build drawing has CON-012 only; the listing also has CON-001, passengers and STK-001.`,from:`Your diagram matches the checked model: CON-012 is a concern`,to:`What you placed matches the checked model: CON-012 is a concern`},{why:`The SysON picture has both concerns and no defined-by line, so it is not “the same elements, joined the same way”.`,from:`Compare it with the diagram you built earlier in this section: the same elements, joined the same way.`,to:`Your Build diagram drew one of these concerns; this model has both. SysON writes each stakeholder’s definition after the colon, where the course figure draws a defined-by line.`},{why:`The stage draws the value in full, so nothing is shortened.`,from:`beside the lines are not part of the notation, and the long text value is shortened to “power…”.`,to:`beside the lines are not part of the notation.`},{why:`As the row above.`,from:` The compartment shortens the text to “power…”.`,to:``},{why:`The doc comment on context is in the listing and was not disclosed as undrawn.`,from:`The usages could also be listed in a parts compartment of context; drawing each as its own node shows its definition.`,to:`The usages could also be listed in a parts compartment of context; drawing each as its own node shows its definition. Course-simplified: the doc comment on context is not drawn.`},{why:`The corrected drawing writes maxWaitP90 = 15 [min] without its type, DurationValue.`,from:`Course-simplified: subject and stakeholder compartments are omitted, and the constraint node`,to:`Course-simplified: subject and stakeholder compartments and the type of maxWaitP90 are omitted, and the constraint node`},{why:`A stakeholder usage is labeled STK too, and statement 2 tests that, so “the ENT and STK boxes” could trap a careful reader.`,from:`and the ENT and STK boxes are part definitions.`,to:`and the square-cornered ENT and STK boxes are part definitions.`},{why:`The drawing has a «stakeholder» node, not a stakeholders compartment.`,from:`Who holds each concern is in the stakeholders compartment.`,to:`Who holds each concern is in the «stakeholder» node joined to it.`},{why:`Week 2 step 1 is about entering the trip, not opening the app.`,from:`Step 1: the passenger opens the app`,to:`Step 1: the passenger enters the origin, destination and party size`},{why:`The course figure also draws the actor as its own stick figure. Only the objective differs.`,from:`The course figure follows the specification’s compartments, and SysON draws the actor and the objective as their own nodes.`,to:`The course figure writes the objective in a compartment, and SysON draws it as its own node.`},{why:`No student-accessible SysON exists yet, so the tutorial must not imply one. Directive: do not claim Phase II is student-accessible.`,from:`Open SysON at the address your instructor gives you.`,to:`If your instructor has a SysON session for your class, open it at the address you are given.`},{why:`SysON work is not submitted or graded, so the old reason contradicted the rest of the tutorial.`,from:`so that each model can be exported and checked on its own. Give it this exact name; your instructor can then find it.`,to:`so that each model stays separate. Use this exact name, so your screen matches the pictures in these steps.`},{why:`It sat in a “Do this” block and said there was nothing to do. Now it also warns the student off.`,from:`Read this step; there is nothing to do. The course team forced a second subject`,to:`Read only: do not repeat this on your model. The course team forced a second subject`},{why:`A first-timer should not read dozens of warnings as a failure.`,from:` warnings on this correct model. Each one has been traced:`,to:` warnings on this correct model, and that is expected. Each one has been traced:`},{why:`American usage.`,from:`Your SysON work is not marked and the app cannot see it.`,to:`Your SysON work is not graded and the app cannot see it.`},{why:`SysON is optional and a student can open it before Build, so it must not open by saying the student has built anything.`,from:`You have read and built the diagram here. Now build the same model in SysON,`,to:`This optional tutorial builds the same model in SysON,`},{why:`The answer in section 4 is the cause, and the report names another element.`,from:`Click the element where the OMG Pilot Implementation reports the error.`,to:`Click the element that breaks the rule.`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`ran the course checker on the file`,to:`ran the OMG Pilot on the file`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`the lab tools draft and the course checker checks`,to:`the lab tools draft and the OMG Pilot checks`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`The OMG checker rejected`,to:`The OMG Pilot rejected`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`separate from the OMG checker`,to:`separate from the OMG Pilot`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`The export then fails the course checker with`,to:`The OMG Pilot then rejects the export with`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`The checker reads it correctly.`,to:`The OMG Pilot reads it correctly.`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`and the checker reads it that way.`,to:`and the OMG Pilot reads it that way.`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`the checker reads them correctly.`,to:`the OMG Pilot reads them correctly.`},{why:`One name for one tool.`,from:`so only the OMG checker can count them.`,to:`so only the OMG Pilot can count them.`},{why:`The prompt repeated its own word.`,from:`What kind of element is weather, the weather in this context?`,to:`What kind of element is weather in this context?`}],r={"~ChargingPort is a different port definition from ChargingPort.":!0};function i(e){if(typeof e==`string`)return n.reduce((e,t)=>e.replaceAll(t.from,t.to),e);if(Array.isArray(e))return e.map(i);if(!e||typeof e!=`object`)return e;let t=Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(e).map(([e,t])=>[e,i(t)]));return typeof t.text==`string`&&t.text in r?{...t,correct:r[t.text]}:t}var a=e=>i(e),o=[{rules:[`checkFeatureSubobjectSpecialization`,`checkFeatureSuboccurrenceSpecialization`],sections:[1,2],text:`False alarm. A part nested in a part is a subobject and a suboccurrence, but this check looks the library feature up under a misspelled name, so it cannot succeed on your part.`},{rules:[`checkItemUsageSubitemSpecialization`],sections:[1,2],text:`False alarm. The library feature is called subitems, but the check asks for subitem. Your nested part does specialize subitems.`},{rules:[`checkFeatureSuboccurrenceSpecialization`,`checkOccurrenceUsageSuboccurrenceSpecialization`],sections:[4],text:`SysON storage. SysON stored your stakeholder as composite, although a parameter is a reference. The warning describes that stored flag, not your model’s meaning.`},{rules:[`checkFeatureParameterRedefinition`],sections:[1,2,4],text:`False alarm on elements with no owner type: the rule concerns parameters inside a behavior, so it does not apply to them. In section 4 the subject and stakeholder rows have a different cause: the tool leaves the parameter’s direction blank, although the language makes a subject an input. The checker reads it correctly.`},{rules:[`validateRequirementUsageSubjectParameterPosition`],sections:[4],text:`SysON storage. The tool leaves the subject’s direction blank, although the language makes a subject an input. The subject is first in your model, and the checker reads it that way.`},{rules:[`validateRequirementUsageOnlyOneSubject`],sections:[4],text:`False alarm. The language says your subject redefines the library’s subj, so there is one subject. SysON does not derive that link and counts both.`},{rules:[`validateSubjectMembershipOwningType`],sections:[4],text:`False alarm. A concern may own a subject. This rule contains a misspelled operation, so it cannot return true.`},{rules:[`validateUsageIsReferential`],sections:[1,2,4],text:`SysON storage. A usage directly in a package counts as referential when read from text. The click tool stored it as composite, so the warning describes SysON’s store, not your text.`},{rules:[`validatePartUsagePartDefinition`],sections:[1,2],text:`False alarm. Your part is implicitly typed by the library’s Part, which is a part definition. SysON looks only at a typing you wrote yourself.`},{rules:[`checkStepOwnedPerformanceSpecialization`],sections:[2,4],text:`False alarm. The rule’s expression has an unbalanced parenthesis. When corrected, it holds for your model.`},{rules:[`checkFeatureSpecialization`,`checkFeatureObjectSpecialization`,`checkFeatureOccurrenceSpecialization`,`checkAttributeUsageSpecialization`,`checkFeatureDataValueSpecialization`],sections:[2,4],text:`False alarm. The language gives these elements a link to a general library feature automatically. The OMG Pilot supplies it, and SysON’s calculation omits it for this kind of element.`},{rules:[`checkExpressionSpecialization`,`checkLiteralExpressionSpecialization`,`checkLiteralIntegerSpecialization`,`checkLiteralInfinitySpecialization`,`checkLiteralStringSpecialization`,`checkStepSpecialization`,`checkTypeSpecialization`,`validateExpressionResultParameterMembership`,`checkExpressionTypeFeaturing`,`checkFeatureValueBindingConnector`,`validateMultiplicityRangeBoundResultTypes`,`validateFeatureMultiplicityDomain`],sections:[2],text:`False alarm. Values such as 4 and [0..*] get implied library links under the language rules. SysON does not compute them for literals, so these checks see an empty element.`},{rules:[`validateExpressionResultParameterMembership`],sections:[4],text:`False alarm. A concern has one result, because its own result redefines the library one. SysON counts both.`},{rules:[`validateSubsettingFeaturingTypes`],sections:[2],text:`SysON limitation. SysON’s accessibility check is unfinished and always answers no. Your feature is accessible, and the OMG Pilot confirms it.`},{rules:[`checkFeatureObjectSpecialization`,`checkFeatureOccurrenceSpecialization`,`checkFeatureSpecialization`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm. The language links your subject to general library features automatically. SysON does not compute those links for a subject.`},{rules:[`checkFeatureParameterRedefinition`],sections:[7],text:`Several causes. On the use case and view1 the rule concerns parameters inside a behavior, so it does not apply. On the objective it does not apply either, because an objective is not a parameter. On enterprise and passengers the tool leaves the direction blank, though the language makes subjects and actors inputs; the checker reads them correctly.`},{rules:[`validateCaseUsageSubjectParameterPosition`],sections:[7],text:`SysON storage. The tool leaves the subject’s direction blank, though the language makes it an input. The subject is first in your model, and the checker reads it that way.`},{rules:[`checkFeatureSuboccurrenceSpecialization`],sections:[7],text:`SysON storage. SysON stored your actor as composite. An actor is a reference parameter, so the warning describes SysON’s stored flag, not your model’s meaning.`},{rules:[`checkOccurrenceUsageSuboccurrenceSpecialization`],sections:[7],text:`On passengers, the same stored flag as above. On the objective, a false alarm: an objective is implied to be a suboccurrence of the use case, and SysON does not compute that link.`},{rules:[`checkRequirementUsageObjectiveRedefinition`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm. The language makes your objective redefine the library’s obj. SysON does not resolve that library objective, so this check fails on any use case.`},{rules:[`checkStepEnclosedPerformanceSpecialization`,`checkStepSubperformanceSpecialization`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm. The rule uses a singular library name where the library’s name is plural, and SysON also omits the implied link. It cannot pass for any objective in this version.`},{rules:[`checkStepOwnedPerformanceSpecialization`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm. The rule’s expression has an unbalanced parenthesis. When corrected, it does not apply to a use case directly in a package.`},{rules:[`validateCaseUsageOnlyOneObjective`,`validateCaseUsageOnlyOneSubject`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm, and do not use it as a test. SysON counts yours plus the library’s inherited one, so it reports two when you made one. It reports the same warning if you really made two, so only the OMG checker can count them.`},{rules:[`validateExpressionResultParameterMembership`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm. An objective has one result, because the requirement’s result redefines the library one. SysON counts both.`},{rules:[`validateObjectiveMembershipOwningType`,`validateSubjectMembershipOwningType`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm. A use case may own an objective and a subject. This rule contains a misspelled operation, so it cannot return true.`},{rules:[`validateRequirementUsageSubjectParameterPosition`],sections:[7],text:`False alarm. Your objective inherits its subject subj, which is its first input, so the rule holds. SysON looks only for a subject you made on the objective.`},{rules:[`validateUsageIsReferential`],sections:[7],text:`SysON storage. A usage directly in a package counts as referential when read from text. The click tool stored it as composite, so the warning describes SysON’s store.`}],s=e=>o.filter(t=>t.sections.includes(e)),c={native:{label:`Native SysML v2 construct`,text:`A keyword or notation defined by the SysML v2 language itself.`},library:{label:`Model-library definition`,text:`A definition from a SysML v2 standard model library (for example ISQ, Views or RiskMetadata), used through an import.`},pattern:{label:`Course modeling pattern`,text:`A convention this course chose, written with native constructs. Another team or tool may choose differently.`},tool:{label:`Tool-specific view`,text:`How a modeling tool chooses to draw or lay out model content. The specification does not fix it.`},conceptual:{label:`Conceptual diagram (not SysML)`,text:`A Systems Thinking diagram with no SysML v2 construct behind it. It stays a document or a comment.`}},l=e({CLASSIFICATION:()=>c,CONCEPTUAL_ARTIFACTS:()=>m,LEARN_INTRO:()=>d,LEARN_SECTIONS:()=>p,REVIEWED_SECTIONS:()=>f}),u=e=>`lectures/week08/${e}.sysml`,d=`In Weeks 1 to 7 you built the Phase I artifacts and learned what each one means. This page does not repeat that. For each artifact it shows how SysML v2 represents it, which constructs you use, a listing the OMG Pilot Implementation has checked, the graphical notation where the specification defines one, what the model then means, which relationships are valid, and the readings students most often get wrong. AeroLink Urban Mobility is the course’s fictional worked case: every reference ID below is one you met in Phase I.`,f=[{id:`definitions`,title:`The context: definitions and usages`,known:{week:1,text:`The context diagram: the enterprise inside its boundary and the external systems around it.`},representation:`Each kind of thing becomes a definition (part def), a reusable type. Each thing that appears in the context becomes a usage (part) defined by one of those definitions. The context itself is a part usage that owns those usages.`,constructs:[{text:`part def Name;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.11.2 Part Definitions and Usages, p.92`,cite:{reading:`R81`,pages:`92`}},{text:`part name : Definition;  (the colon reads “defined by”)`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.6 Definition and Usage, pp.63–64`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`63–64`}},{text:`Short name <'EXT-002'>`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.2 Elements and Relationships, p.48`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`48`}},{text:`doc /* … */`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.4 Annotations, p.51`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`51`}},{text:`Every Phase I ID written as a short name; the context written as one part usage`,kind:`pattern`,clause:`Course convention (Weeks 8 to 13)`}],file:u(`learn_01_definition_usage`),focus:[{line:5,text:`A definition: the enterprise as a type, with its Phase I ID as the short name.`},{line:10,text:`A usage: the enterprise as it appears in this context, defined by ENT-001.`},{line:11,text:`A usage with its own short name, defined by the external-system definition.`}],figure:{id:`definition-usage`,kind:`native`,caption:`General view of the listing. A definition is a rectangle with square corners; a usage has rounded corners and shows “name : Definition”. A line with a filled diamond at context marks each usage as one of its parts; a line ending in two dots and a hollow triangle marks “defined by”. Course aid: the words “defined by” beside those lines are not part of the notation. The usages could also be listed in a parts compartment of context; drawing each as its own node shows its definition.`},meaning:`The model now says: there is one kind of thing called AeroLink Urban Mobility, and the context contains exactly one usage of it, named enterprise. The electric grid and the weather are separate definitions, each used once in the context. Nothing is said yet about how they interact; that comes with interfaces. Course simplification: Week 1 draws the external systems around the air-taxi service; here the enterprise stands in for it.`,relationships:[{text:`A part usage is defined by a part definition (part enterprise : ENT-001).`,valid:!0,why:`Usages are typed by definitions.`},{text:`A part usage is defined by another part usage (part enterprise : aeroLink).`,valid:!1,why:`The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects it: an occurrence, item or part must be typed by occurrence definitions. Use subsets to relate two usages.`},{text:`A part usage owns other part usages (part context { part enterprise : … }).`,valid:!0,why:`Nesting a usage inside a usage makes it one of that usage’s parts.`}],invalid:{file:u(`invalid_learn_part_typed_by_usage`),text:`A usage typed by another usage. The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects line 6.`},readings:[{text:`“part def ENT-001” says that AeroLink exists in the context.`,correct:!1,why:`A definition is only a type. The usage part enterprise inside context is what places AeroLink in the context.`},{text:`“part enterprise : ENT-001” is one usage of the definition ENT-001.`,correct:!0,why:`The colon reads “defined by”: the usage enterprise is defined by ENT-001.`},{text:`The short name <'EXT-002'> and the name electricGrid are two different elements.`,correct:!1,why:`They identify one element: either can be used to refer to it.`}],aerolink:[`ENT-001`,`EXT-002`,`EXT-003`],diagram:{readings:[{text:`The square-cornered boxes are definitions and the rounded boxes are usages: 'Electric grid' is a definition and electricGrid is a usage.`,correct:!0,why:`SysML v2 draws a definition with square corners and a usage with rounded corners. «part def» marks a part definition, «part» a part usage.`},{text:`The box «part def» <'ENT-001'> 'AeroLink Urban Mobility' is what puts the enterprise in the operating context.`,correct:!1,why:`A definition is only a reusable type: on its own it is in no context. The usage enterprise, the rounded box under context, is what puts the enterprise in this context.`},{text:`The lines with a filled diamond at context say that enterprise, electricGrid and weather are parts of context.`,correct:!0,why:`A filled diamond at the owner marks a composite feature: each of the three usages is a part of context. A white diamond would mean a reference, not a part.`},{text:`electricGrid and 'Electric grid' are two names of one element.`,correct:!1,why:`They are two elements joined by a defined-by line: the usage electricGrid in this context, and the definition 'Electric grid' that says what kind of thing it is. The short name <'EXT-002'> and the name electricGrid, on the other hand, do name one element.`}],mistake:{file:u(`invalid_learn_part_typed_by_usage`),figure:`definition-usage`,text:`This diagram draws an intentionally invalid model. One element breaks a SysML v2 rule. Click the element where the OMG Pilot Implementation reports the error.`,accept:[{line:6,text:`Found it. The part enterprise is defined by aeroLink, which is a usage (rounded corners), not a definition. The OMG Pilot Implementation reports: “An occurrence, item or part must be typed by occurrence definitions.” A part usage must be defined by a part definition: here enterprise : 'ENT-001'.`}],miss:`Follow each defined-by line and look at the corners of the box it ends at.`},build:{figure:`definition-usage`,text:`Add the third external system from the Week 1 context diagram: the weather, EXT-003. Choose the kind of each element, what weather is defined by, and which element owns it. The diagram redraws as you choose.`,slots:[{id:`def-kind`,label:`What kind of element is 'Weather', the kind of external system?`,options:[{id:`partdef`,text:`part def`,ok:!0,feedback:`A kind of thing is a definition: a reusable type, drawn square-cornered.`},{id:`part`,text:`part`,ok:!1,feedback:`A part is a usage: one occurrence in a context. The kind of external system is a definition.`}]},{id:`use-kind`,label:`What kind of element is weather, the weather in this context?`,options:[{id:`part`,text:`part`,ok:!0,feedback:`The weather as it appears in this context is a usage, drawn rounded.`},{id:`partdef`,text:`part def`,ok:!1,feedback:`A definition only says what kind of thing something is. The weather in this context is a usage of that definition.`}]},{id:`type`,label:`weather is defined by:`,options:[{id:`weather`,text:`'Weather'`,ok:!0,feedback:`The usage weather is defined by the part definition 'Weather'.`},{id:`grid`,text:`electricGrid`,ok:!1,feedback:`electricGrid is a usage. A part usage must be defined by a part definition: the mistake in step 4.`},{id:`ent`,text:`'ENT-001'`,ok:!1,feedback:`ENT-001 defines the enterprise. The weather is a different kind of thing, with its own definition.`}]},{id:`owner`,label:`Which element owns weather, so that it is a part of it?`,options:[{id:`context`,text:`context`,ok:!0,feedback:`weather is one of the parts of context, like enterprise and electricGrid: a filled diamond at context.`},{id:`def`,text:`'Weather'`,ok:!1,feedback:`A definition defines its usages; it does not own them. The usage belongs to the context it appears in.`}]}],done:`Your diagram matches the checked model: 'Weather' is a part definition, and weather is a part of context defined by it.`,lines:[7,8,12]},elements:[{at:8,name:`«part» context`,means:`A part usage that stands for the operating context of Week 1: the enterprise together with the external systems around it.`,why:`It is drawn rounded because it is a usage. It owns the three usages below it, the filled-diamond lines, so each of them is one of its parts. It has no definition of its own here: the course only needs this one context.`},{at:10,name:`«part» enterprise : 'ENT-001'`,means:`The enterprise as it appears in this context.`,why:`It is a part of context (the filled-diamond line from context) and is defined by the part definition ENT-001 (the defined-by line). The colon in enterprise : 'ENT-001' reads “defined by”.`},{at:11,name:`«part» <'EXT-002'> electricGrid : 'Electric grid'`,means:`The electric grid as it appears in this context, with its Week 1 ID EXT-002 as the short name.`,why:`A part of context, defined by the part definition 'Electric grid'. The short name is on the usage because EXT-002 identifies this external system in this context.`},{at:12,name:`«part» <'EXT-003'> weather : 'Weather'`,means:`The weather as it appears in this context, Week 1 ID EXT-003.`,why:`A part of context, defined by the part definition 'Weather'.`},{at:5,name:`«part def» <'ENT-001'> 'AeroLink Urban Mobility'`,means:`The part definition of the enterprise, with its Phase I ID ENT-001: the kind of thing AeroLink is.`,why:`It is square-cornered because it is a definition. It is not inside context: a definition is a reusable type, and the usage enterprise is what places it in the context. Later sections define stakeholders, concerns and requirements about this same definition.`},{at:6,name:`«part def» 'Electric grid'`,means:`The part definition of an electric grid: what kind of thing electricGrid is.`,why:`electricGrid is defined by it. Another context could have its own usage of the same definition.`},{at:7,name:`«part def» 'Weather'`,means:`The part definition of the weather: what kind of thing weather is.`,why:`weather is defined by it.`}],tool:{title:`Now build it in SysON`,intro:`You have read and built the diagram here. Now build the same model in SysON, the graphical SysML v2 modeling tool the course selected for Week 8: three part definitions and the context with its three usages, by clicking and filling in fields only. You do not type any SysML. Each step says what to do, why, and what your screen should look like. Open SysON at the address your instructor gives you. This tutorial is practice in the tool. It is not your team’s enterprise model and it is not submitted: your enterprise model is the .sysml file the lab tools draft and the course checker checks.`,steps:[{title:`Create the project AeroLink Week 8 Section 1`,text:`On the SysON home page, click the SysMLv2 card under Create a new project. Type the name AeroLink Week 8 Section 1 and click CREATE PROJECT. Then click Open view1.`,why:`Each Week 8 section has its own SysON project, so that each model can be exported and checked on its own. Give it this exact name; your instructor can then find it.`,result:`An empty General View canvas named view1. In the Explorer on the left, SysMLv2.sysml holds one package, Package1.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u01-new-project-form-sysmlv2-template.png`,alt:`The Create a new project form with the name AeroLink Week 8 Section 1 typed in and the SysMLv2 template chosen.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u02-empty-general-view-view1.png`,alt:`The empty General View canvas, with SysMLv2.sysml and Package1 in the Explorer.`}]},{title:`Rename the package`,text:`Click Package1 in the Explorer. In Details on the right, set Declared Name to L1 Definitions and usages.`,why:`A package is the container for the model. Its name is how the elements inside it will be found.`,result:`The Explorer and Details both show the package as L1 Definitions and usages.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u03-rename-root-package.png`,alt:`Details showing the Declared Name L1 Definitions and usages, and the renamed package in the Explorer.`}]},{title:`Create the three part definitions`,text:`Right-click empty canvas and choose Structure, then New Part Definition. In Details set Declared Name to AeroLink Urban Mobility and Declared Short Name to ENT-001. Do the same twice more, with the names Electric grid and Weather and no short name.`,why:`Definitions first. A definition is the reusable kind of thing, and a usage needs one to be defined by. Each one is created on the empty canvas because a definition does not belong to the context.`,result:`Three square-cornered boxes marked «part def». The first shows <ENT-001> AeroLink Urban Mobility.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u04-palette-structure-new-part-definition.png`,alt:`The canvas palette open on Structure, with New Part Definition highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u05-partdef-ENT-001-AeroLink-Urban-Mobility.png`,alt:`A square-cornered «part def» node <ENT-001> AeroLink Urban Mobility, with its name and short name in Details.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u07-partdef-Weather.png`,alt:`Three «part def» nodes on the canvas, the newest named Weather.`}]},{title:`Create the part usage context`,text:`Right-click empty canvas and choose Structure, then New Part. Set Declared Name to context. In Documentation type: The operating context of Week 1: the enterprise and two of its six external systems. Press Tab to commit it.`,why:`This time the tool is New Part, not New Part Definition: context is a usage. It stands for one operating context, the Week 1 context diagram. Leave Typed by empty, as in the checked model.`,result:`A rounded box marked «part» named context. Rounded corners mean a usage; compare them with the square corners of the definitions.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u08-palette-structure-new-part.png`,alt:`The canvas palette open on Structure, with New Part highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u09-part-context-documentation.png`,alt:`A rounded «part» context node, with the full sentence in Documentation in Details.`}]},{title:`Add enterprise inside context`,text:`Right-click the context node itself, near its left edge, and choose Structure, then New Part. Set Declared Name to enterprise. Click Typed by, type AeroLink, and pick AeroLink Urban Mobility from the list.`,why:`Starting from the context node is what makes enterprise one of its parts. Typed by is SysON’s name for “defined by”. Its list offers definitions only, so here you cannot make the mistake from step 4.`,result:`A filled-diamond line from context to enterprise, and a line with a hollow triangle to the part definition. SysON labels the usage enterprise : AeroLink Urban Mobility, using the definition’s name; the diagram in this course shows its short name, ENT-001. Both are the same definition.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u10-context-node-palette-structure-new-part.png`,alt:`The context node’s own palette open on Structure, with New Part highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u11-enterprise-typed-by-autocomplete.png`,alt:`A filled-diamond line joins context to the new node enterprise. The Typed by list offers only AeroLink Urban Mobility.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u12-part-enterprise-typed-ENT-001.png`,alt:`The node now reads enterprise : AeroLink Urban Mobility, with a typing line to the part definition.`}]},{title:`Add electricGrid inside context`,text:`Right-click the context node again, near its left edge, and choose Structure, then New Part. Set Declared Name to electricGrid and Declared Short Name to EXT-002. In Typed by, pick Electric grid.`,why:`The same pattern again: one usage in the context, defined by its own definition. The short name goes on the usage because EXT-002 identifies this external system in the Week 1 context.`,result:`A second filled-diamond line from context, and the node <EXT-002> electricGrid : Electric grid.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u13-electricGrid-typed-by-autocomplete.png`,alt:`The new node electricGrid with the short name EXT-002, and the Typed by list offering only Electric grid.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u14-part-electricGrid-EXT-002-typed.png`,alt:`The node <EXT-002> electricGrid : Electric grid, with its typing line to the part definition.`}]},{title:`Add weather and draw its defined-by line`,text:`Add a third part inside context: Declared Name weather, Declared Short Name EXT-003. This time, select weather, drag from the handle at its edge and drop on the Weather part definition. In the list that appears, click New Feature Typing.`,why:`This is the second way to set a definition: draw the line. Take care where you drop it. The list in Typed by offers definitions only, but the drag does not: when the course team dropped the line on a usage, SysON accepted it with no warning, and the OMG Pilot Implementation rejected the exported model. That is the mistake from step 4. If the label shows a usage after the colon, delete the typing and draw it again.`,result:`The node <EXT-003> weather : Weather with a line to the part definition, and Weather in Typed by in Details.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u15-weather-drag-edge-palette-new-feature-typing.png`,alt:`The list that appears after dragging from weather, with New Feature Typing at the bottom of it.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u16-part-weather-EXT-003-typed-by-edge.png`,alt:`The node <EXT-003> weather : Weather, with Weather shown in Typed by in Details.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-m04-after-new-feature-typing-to-usage.png`,alt:`A separate test project showing the mistake: the node reads enterprise : aeroLink, a usage after the colon, no defined-by line is drawn and Typed by in Details is empty.`}]},{title:`Arrange the diagram and read it`,text:`Click empty canvas. In the toolbar above the canvas, click Arrange all, then Fit to screen. Compare the diagram with the one you built in this app. Right-click context and open Show/Hide to see the options, then close the list without choosing one.`,why:`The layout means nothing to the model, but a tidy diagram is easier to check. Show/Hide offers another way to see the same ownership: Show content as Nested lists a part inside a parts compartment of context, instead of as its own node with a diamond line.`,result:`context at the top, three filled-diamond lines down to the three rounded usages, and a line with a hollow triangle from each usage to its square-cornered definition: the same structure as the diagram in this app.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u17-arrange-all-fit-whole-model.png`,alt:`The whole model arranged: context at the top, three usages below it joined by filled-diamond lines, and three part definitions below them.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u18-zoom-ownership-and-typing-notation.png`,alt:`A close-up of the filled diamonds at context and the hollow-triangle arrowheads at the definitions.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u19-context-palette-show-hide.png`,alt:`The Show/Hide list of the context node, including Show content as Nested.`}]},{title:`Confirm the model is saved`,text:`SysON saves each change as you make it, so there is no Save button. Close the browser tab. Open SysON again, open AeroLink Week 8 Section 1 from the project list, and open view1. Click context and then electricGrid in the Explorer.`,why:`Reopening proves that your work is in the model, not only on your screen. Check the values that are easiest to lose: the documentation, the short name and the definition.`,result:`All seven elements are back. context still has its Documentation; electricGrid still has the short name EXT-002 and Electric grid in Typed by.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u23-reopened-diagram-and-explorer.png`,alt:`The reopened project with all seven nodes on the canvas and the Explorer tree expanded.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u24-reopened-context-details-documentation.png`,alt:`Details for context after reopening, with its Documentation still set.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u25-reopened-electricGrid-details-shortname-typedby.png`,alt:`Details for electricGrid after reopening: short name EXT-002 and Typed by Electric grid.`}]},{title:`Inspect the completed model`,text:`Open the ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml in the Explorer and choose Expand all. Read the tree from top to bottom against the checklist below. Then click the warning triangle on the left edge to open the Validation panel.`,why:`The Explorer shows every element, including ones the diagram does not draw. Check for: three part definitions, with ENT-001 on the first; context with Documentation and three parts; EXT-002 on electricGrid and EXT-003 on weather; each of the three parts with a FeatureTyping child, which is its defined-by link.`,result:`The Validation panel reports 13 warnings on this correct model. Each one has been traced: none is a defect in the model, 12 are false alarms and 1 comes from how SysON stores a part. The list below explains them. If your count differs, compare your model with these steps. The panel is separate from the OMG checker, which is the reference.`,warnings:s(1),imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u21-explorer-tree-expanded.png`,alt:`The expanded Explorer tree: view1, three part definitions, and context with Documentation and three parts, each with a FeatureTyping child.`},{src:`syson/week8/s1-u27-validation-panel.png`,alt:`The Validation panel opened from the warning triangle on the left edge, reporting Warning, 13 diagnostics.`}]},{title:`Optional: see the SysML text`,text:`Open the ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml again and choose Download. Open the downloaded file in any text editor and compare it with the SysML text in step 2 of this section.`,why:`You did not write this text: SysON generated it from your diagram. Reading it shows that a diagram and its text are two views of one model. Nothing here needs editing.`,result:`The same definitions and usages as the listing. SysON adds a view block for its diagram, and writes Weather without quotes because the name needs none.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s1-u26-export-document-menu-download.png`,alt:`The ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml with Download among its commands.`}]}],after:[`What you built: the context of the AeroLink baseline. Three definitions say what kinds of thing exist, and three usages say what is in this context. Each usage is owned by context and defined by one definition.`,`It is the same model as the listing in step 2. When the course team exported this model with SysON’s Download command and ran the course checker on the file, it passed; SysON adds a view block for its diagram and leaves the elements as written.`,`For your team’s enterprise model: use the same pattern. Decide what kinds of thing your enterprise and its external systems are (definitions), then what appears in your context (usages), and give each usage the ID from your Week 1 register as its short name.`,`Your SysON work is not marked and the app cannot see it. SysON’s export of other constructs (requirements, interfaces, viewpoints) still has known faults, so the course does not grade SysON exports.`]}},next:[{week:10,text:`Logical architecture: the logical elements become part usages of the enterprise.`},{week:11,text:`Physical architecture: physical elements are part definitions and usages with multiplicities.`}]},{id:`specialization`,title:`Categories: specialization, subsetting and redefinition`,known:{week:4,text:`Kinds of things in the architecture: external systems of one kind, hub and spoke vertiports.`},representation:`A category of things becomes a general definition; each kind becomes a definition that specializes it (:>). A usage that is one of a collection subsets that collection (subsets). A usage or a specialized definition that fixes an inherited feature redefines it (:>>).`,constructs:[{text:`part def B :> A;  (subclassification, also written specializes)`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.6 Definition and Usage, pp.63–66; notation p.68`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`63–68`}},{text:`part b : B subsets a;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.6 Definition and Usage, p.65; notation p.69`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`65–69`}},{text:`attribute :>> padCount = 4;  (redefinition, also written redefines)`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.6 Definition and Usage, p.65; notation p.69`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`65–69`}},{text:`String, Natural`,kind:`library`,clause:`ScalarValues library (imported)`}],file:u(`learn_02_specialization`),focus:[{line:9,text:`The electric grid is a kind of external system: it inherits crossesBoundary.`},{line:14,text:`A hub vertiport fixes the inherited pad count at 4.`},{line:18,text:`The grid usage is one of the context’s externals.`}],figure:{id:`specialization`,kind:`native`,caption:`General view of the listing. Subclassification is a solid line with a hollow triangle at the general definition; subsetting uses the same triangle between two usages; a redefined feature is shown in the compartment as “:>> name”. Course aids: the words “specializes”, “subsets” and “defined by” beside the lines are not part of the notation, and the long text value is shortened to “power…”.`},meaning:`Every hub vertiport is a vertiport with exactly 4 pads. Every electric grid is an external system, so it has a crossesBoundary attribute; in this context the grid usage is one of the externals and states what crosses the boundary.`,relationships:[{text:`A definition specializes a definition (HubVertiport :> Vertiport).`,valid:!0,why:`Subclassification relates two definitions.`},{text:`A usage subsets a usage (electricGrid subsets externals).`,valid:!0,why:`Subsetting relates two usages.`},{text:`A usage redefines a feature its owner does not inherit.`,valid:!1,why:`A redefinition must name an inherited feature; otherwise the name does not resolve.`}],invalid:{file:u(`invalid_learn_redefine_not_inherited`),text:`A redefinition with nothing to redefine. The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects line 14.`},readings:[{text:`HubVertiport :> Vertiport means a hub vertiport contains a vertiport.`,correct:!1,why:`It means every hub vertiport IS a vertiport (a kind of), not that it contains one.`},{text:`:>> padCount = 4 changes the pad count of every Vertiport.`,correct:!1,why:`Only HubVertiport and its specializations are constrained; Vertiport still allows any Natural.`},{text:`subsets externals means the grid is one of the externals of this context.`,correct:!0,why:`Every value of electricGrid is also a value of externals.`}],aerolink:[`EXT-002`],diagram:{readings:[{text:`The line from HubVertiport to Vertiport has its hollow triangle at Vertiport, so Vertiport is the general definition.`,correct:!0,why:`The triangle always sits at the general element. HubVertiport is the specialized one: every hub vertiport is a vertiport.`},{text:`The triangle line between HubVertiport and Vertiport means a hub vertiport contains a vertiport.`,correct:!1,why:`Containment would be a line with a filled diamond, as between context and its usages. The triangle says “is a kind of”: a hub vertiport IS a vertiport.`},{text:`The triangle line from electricGrid to externals says the grid is one of the externals of this context.`,correct:!0,why:`Both ends are rounded, so both are usages, and between usages the triangle is subsetting: every value of electricGrid is also a value of externals.`},{text:`The compartment entry :>> padCount = 4 in HubVertiport sets the pad count of every Vertiport.`,correct:!1,why:`The entry belongs to HubVertiport. Vertiport still has padCount : Natural, so another vertiport can have any number of pads. :>> marks a feature redefined here, not a new one.`}],mistake:{file:u(`invalid_learn_redefine_not_inherited`),figure:`specialization`,text:`This diagram draws an intentionally invalid model. One element breaks a SysML v2 rule. Click the element where the OMG Pilot Implementation reports the error.`,accept:[{line:14,text:`Found it. :>> padCount redefines an inherited feature, but HubVertiport inherits nothing: no triangle joins it to Vertiport. The OMG Pilot Implementation reports: “Couldn't resolve reference to Feature 'padCount'.” The diagram needs a line with a hollow triangle from HubVertiport to Vertiport.`},{line:13,text:`Found it. HubVertiport has no triangle to Vertiport, so it inherits nothing and its entry :>> padCount has nothing to redefine. The OMG Pilot Implementation reports on that entry: “Couldn't resolve reference to Feature 'padCount'.” The diagram needs a line with a hollow triangle from HubVertiport to Vertiport.`}],miss:`Compare the two pairs of definitions. A :>> entry needs an inherited feature to redefine.`},build:{figure:`specialization`,text:`Finish the diagram: make HubVertiport a kind of vertiport with exactly 4 pads, and place the electric grid among the externals of the context. The diagram redraws as you choose.`,slots:[{id:`general`,label:`A hub vertiport is a kind of vertiport. HubVertiport’s line with a hollow triangle ends at:`,options:[{id:`vertiport`,text:`Vertiport`,ok:!0,feedback:`The triangle sits at the general definition, so HubVertiport inherits padCount.`},{id:`external`,text:`ExternalSystem`,ok:!1,feedback:`A hub vertiport is not an external system. Specialize the definition whose features it inherits.`},{id:`externals`,text:`externals`,ok:!1,feedback:`externals is a usage (rounded). Subclassification relates two definitions.`}]},{id:`pad-form`,label:`HubVertiport’s attributes compartment reads:`,options:[{id:`redef`,text:`:>> padCount = 4`,ok:!0,feedback:`:>> redefines the inherited padCount and fixes it at 4 for hub vertiports only.`},{id:`new`,text:`padCount = 4`,ok:!1,feedback:`Without :>> this declares a second padCount. The OMG Pilot Implementation warns: “Duplicate of inherited member name.”`},{id:`plain`,text:`padCount : Natural`,ok:!1,feedback:`This repeats the general definition and fixes nothing: a hub vertiport must have exactly 4 pads.`}]},{id:`sub-target`,label:`The electric grid is one of the externals. electricGrid’s line with a hollow triangle ends at:`,options:[{id:`externals`,text:`externals`,ok:!0,feedback:`Between two usages the triangle is subsetting: the grid is one of the externals.`},{id:`external`,text:`ExternalSystem`,ok:!1,feedback:`ExternalSystem is a definition. A usage subsets another usage. The OMG Pilot Implementation cannot resolve a definition there.`},{id:`context`,text:`context`,ok:!1,feedback:`context owns electricGrid through the filled-diamond line. Subsetting would say that the grid is a kind of context.`}]},{id:`grid-type`,label:`electricGrid is defined by:`,options:[{id:`grid`,text:`'Electric grid'`,ok:!0,feedback:`The usage is defined by the part definition 'Electric grid', and 'Electric grid' specializes ExternalSystem.`},{id:`externals`,text:`externals`,ok:!1,feedback:`externals is a usage, and a part usage must be defined by a part definition: the mistake from section 1.`}]}],done:`Your diagram matches the checked model: HubVertiport specializes Vertiport and fixes padCount at 4, and electricGrid is defined by 'Electric grid' and subsets externals.`,lines:[13,14,18]},elements:[{at:13,on:`specializes`,name:`Line with a hollow triangle from HubVertiport to Vertiport`,means:`Subclassification: every hub vertiport is a vertiport.`,why:`The triangle sits at the general definition. Through this line HubVertiport inherits padCount.`},{at:9,on:`specializes`,name:`Line with a hollow triangle from 'Electric grid' to ExternalSystem`,means:`Subclassification: every electric grid is an external system.`,why:`Through this line 'Electric grid' inherits crossesBoundary, so the usage electricGrid has it too.`},{at:18,on:`subsets`,name:`Line with a hollow triangle from electricGrid to externals`,means:`Subsetting: the grid is one of the externals of this context.`,why:`It is the same triangle, between two usages. Every value of electricGrid is also a value of externals.`},{at:17,on:`defined by`,name:`Defined-by line from externals to ExternalSystem`,means:`The usage externals is defined by ExternalSystem.`,why:`Each thing in the collection is an external system.`},{at:18,on:`defined by`,name:`Defined-by line from electricGrid to 'Electric grid'`,means:`The usage electricGrid is defined by 'Electric grid'.`,why:`Subsetting and defined-by say different things: externals is the collection the grid belongs to, and the definition is the kind of thing it is.`},{at:17,on:`filled diamond`,name:`Line with a filled diamond from context to externals`,means:`externals is a part of context.`,why:`The filled diamond sits at the owner, as in section 1.`},{at:18,on:`filled diamond`,name:`Line with a filled diamond from context to electricGrid`,means:`electricGrid is a part of context.`,why:`The filled diamond sits at the owner, as in section 1. The grid is both owned by context and one of its externals.`},{at:11,name:`Attribute padCount : Natural`,means:`Every vertiport has a pad count, a natural number.`,why:`The value is left open here, and Natural comes from the ScalarValues library.`},{at:14,name:`Attribute :>> padCount = 4`,means:`HubVertiport redefines the inherited padCount and fixes it at 4.`,why:`:>> marks a redefinition. It works only because the triangle line gives HubVertiport a padCount to inherit.`},{at:7,name:`Attribute crossesBoundary : String`,means:`Every external system says what crosses the boundary, as text.`,why:`String comes from the ScalarValues library. Every definition that specializes ExternalSystem inherits it.`},{at:19,name:`Attribute :>> crossesBoundary = "power to the vertiport chargers"`,means:`This usage states what crosses the boundary for this grid.`,why:`A usage can redefine a feature too: it inherits crossesBoundary through its definition. The compartment shortens the text to “power…”.`},{at:10,name:`«part def» Vertiport`,means:`The general definition of a vertiport.`,why:`It is square-cornered because it is a definition, and it has no usage in this model.`},{at:13,name:`«part def» HubVertiport`,means:`A kind of vertiport with exactly 4 pads.`,why:`It specializes Vertiport and redefines padCount.`},{at:6,name:`«part def» ExternalSystem`,means:`The general definition of an external system.`,why:`It is the general end of the triangle from 'Electric grid', and it defines the collection externals.`},{at:9,name:`«part def» 'Electric grid'`,means:`A kind of external system.`,why:`It adds nothing of its own, and it inherits crossesBoundary.`},{at:16,name:`«part» context`,means:`The operating context, as in section 1.`,why:`It owns the two usages below it.`},{at:17,name:`«part» externals : ExternalSystem [0..*]`,means:`A collection of external systems in this context.`,why:`[0..*] is its multiplicity: any number, including none.`},{at:18,name:`«part» <'EXT-002'> electricGrid : 'Electric grid'`,means:`The electric grid in this context, Week 1 ID EXT-002.`,why:`It is defined by 'Electric grid' and subsets externals, so it is one of the collection.`}],tool:{title:`Now build it in SysON`,intro:`You have read and built the diagram here. Now build the same model in SysON, the graphical SysML v2 modeling tool the course selected for Week 8: four part definitions, the context with its two usages, and the three relationships of this section. You work by clicking and filling in fields. The only things you type are names and values, including the multiplicity [0..*]; you do not write SysML declarations. Each step says what to do, why, and what your screen should look like. Open SysON at the address your instructor gives you. This tutorial is practice in the tool. It is not your team’s enterprise model and it is not submitted: your enterprise model is the .sysml file the lab tools draft and the course checker checks.`,steps:[{title:`Create the project AeroLink Week 8 Section 2`,text:`On the SysON home page, click the SysMLv2 card under Create a new project. Type the name AeroLink Week 8 Section 2 and click CREATE PROJECT. Then click Open view1.`,why:`Each Week 8 section has its own SysON project, so that each model can be exported and checked on its own. Give it this exact name; your instructor can then find it.`,result:`An empty General View canvas named view1. In the Explorer on the left, SysMLv2.sysml holds one package, Package1.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u01-new-project-form-sysmlv2-template.png`,alt:`The Create a new project form with the name AeroLink Week 8 Section 2 typed in and the SysMLv2 template chosen.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u02-empty-general-view-view1.png`,alt:`The empty view1 canvas, with Package1 in the Explorer.`}]},{title:`Rename the package`,text:`Click Package1 in the Explorer. In Details on the right, set Declared Name to L2 Specialization and press Enter.`,why:`A package holds the whole model. The name in the Explorer and in the exported file comes from this field.`,result:`The Explorer and Details both show L2 Specialization.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u03-rename-root-package.png`,alt:`Details and the Explorer showing the package renamed to L2 Specialization.`}]},{title:`Create the four part definitions`,text:`Right-click the empty canvas and choose Structure > New Part Definition. In Details, set Declared Name to ExternalSystem and press Enter. Repeat for Electric grid, Vertiport and HubVertiport. Then click the empty canvas and use Arrange all and Fit to screen in the diagram toolbar.`,why:`These are the kinds of thing in the model. A new node can land at the edge of the canvas, so arrange before you continue.`,result:`Four square-cornered «part def» boxes, all visible.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u04-palette-structure-new-part-definition.png`,alt:`The canvas palette with Structure > New Part Definition highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u09-arrange-all-four-part-defs.png`,alt:`Four square-cornered part definition boxes after Arrange all.`}]},{title:`Add the two general attributes`,text:`Right-click ExternalSystem and choose Structure > New Attribute. Set Declared Name to crossesBoundary. Next to Typed by, click the “…” button, expand ScalarValues twice, click String, click the right arrow and click CLOSE. Do the same on Vertiport: a new attribute padCount, typed by Natural. Then Arrange all and Fit to screen.`,why:`The two general definitions state what every external system and every vertiport has. String and Natural come from the ScalarValues library. The Selected pane in the dialog can stay empty after the arrow click; the type is still saved.`,result:`Each box has an attributes compartment: crossesBoundary : ScalarValues::String and padCount : ScalarValues::Natural.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u10-node-palette-structure-new-attribute.png`,alt:`The ExternalSystem node palette with Structure > New Attribute highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u12-typed-by-dialog-scalarvalues-string.png`,alt:`The Edit reference dialog with String highlighted under ScalarValues.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u15-attributes-in-compartments.png`,alt:`ExternalSystem and Vertiport with their attributes in compartments.`}]},{title:`Draw the two subclassifications`,text:`Select Electric grid, drag from its bottom handle onto ExternalSystem and click New Subclassification. Do the same from HubVertiport onto Vertiport. Then Arrange all and Fit to screen.`,why:`Drag from the specific definition to the general one: the hollow triangle ends at the general definition. SysON’s layout may put the general definition below the specific one. Read the triangle, not the position.`,result:`Two solid lines with hollow triangles. The nodes read Electric grid :> ExternalSystem and HubVertiport :> Vertiport.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u16-drag-edge-palette-new-subclassification.png`,alt:`The small edge palette with New Dependency and New Subclassification.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u18-both-subclassifications-arranged.png`,alt:`Both subclassification lines after Arrange all.`}]},{title:`Redefine padCount in the Explorer`,text:`Open the ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml and choose Expand all. On HubVertiport, open ⋮ > New object > Attribute > CREATE. Do not use New Attribute on the diagram here. On the new attribute1, open ⋮ > New object > Redefinition > CREATE. In Details, click “…” next to Redefined Feature and choose Vertiport > padCount. Click “…” next to Redefining Feature and choose HubVertiport > attribute1.`,why:`Redefinition has no line to draw, so this is the one relationship made in the Explorer. Set both fields: without Redefining Feature the export silently loses the redefinition. Watch for the mistake from step 4 as well: SysON offers padCount even with no triangle, and accepts it without a message. The export then fails the course checker with “Must be an accessible feature (use dot notation for nesting)”. The wording differs from step 4, but the mistake is the same.`,result:`Details lists four filled fields: Subsetted Feature and Redefined Feature are padCount; Subsetting Feature and Redefining Feature are attribute1.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u19-explorer-new-object-attribute-on-HubVertiport.png`,alt:`The Create a new object dialog with Attribute chosen on HubVertiport.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u23-redefined-feature-dialog-Vertiport-padCount.png`,alt:`The Select an object dialog with padCount under Vertiport highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u25-redefinition-both-ends-set.png`,alt:`Details for the Redefinition with all four feature fields filled.`}]},{title:`Fix the value at 4`,text:`Click attribute1 in the Explorer. In Details, click “+” next to Expression value, type 4 and click UPDATE. Then delete all the text in Declared Name and press Enter; do not type a new name. Right-click HubVertiport on the diagram and choose Show/Hide > Show valued content, then Arrange all.`,why:`The value is the only thing you type here. Clearing the name matters because :>> padCount takes its name from the feature it redefines. This attribute was created in the Explorer because a nameless attribute made on the diagram breaks the export.`,result:`HubVertiport’s compartment shows :>> padCount = 4. The Explorer row has an icon but no text.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u26-create-expression-dialog-value-4.png`,alt:`The Create Expression dialog holding the value 4.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u28-name-cleared-explorer-row-blank.png`,alt:`Declared Name empty, with a blank row in the Explorer.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u30-hubvertiport-shows-redefined-padCount-4.png`,alt:`HubVertiport showing the compartment entry :>> padCount = 4.`}]},{title:`Create context and its two usages`,text:`Right-click the empty canvas and choose Structure > New Part. Set Declared Name to context, then Arrange all. Right-click context and choose Structure > New Part. Set Declared Name to externals, type ExternalSystem in Typed by and pick it. Right-click context again for another New Part: set Declared Name to electricGrid and Declared Short Name to EXT-002, type Electric grid in Typed by and pick it.`,why:`You built this structure in section 1. A filled-diamond line appears from context to each part, and Typed by draws the defined-by line.`,result:`Rounded nodes externals : ExternalSystem and <EXT-002> electricGrid : Electric grid under context.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u33-context-node-palette-new-part.png`,alt:`The context node palette with Structure > New Part highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u34-externals-typed-by-autocomplete.png`,alt:`The Typed by suggestions offering ExternalSystem.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u37-electricGrid-EXT-002-typed.png`,alt:`The node <EXT-002> electricGrid : Electric grid under context.`}]},{title:`Set the multiplicity of externals`,text:`Arrange all, then click externals and press F2. Type externals [0..*] (typing replaces the selected text) and press Enter.`,why:`SysON has no field for multiplicity, so it is typed into the node’s label. Only the name and the multiplicity go in: the type stays as you set it.`,result:`The node reads externals[0..*] : ExternalSystem.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u38-f2-label-edit-multiplicity.png`,alt:`The node label open for editing, holding externals [0..*].`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u39-externals-multiplicity-0-star.png`,alt:`The node reading externals[0..*] : ExternalSystem.`}]},{title:`Draw the subsetting`,text:`Arrange all. Select electricGrid, drag from its bottom handle onto externals and click New Subsetting. Then Arrange all and Fit to screen.`,why:`Subsetting draws the same triangle as subclassification. SysON’s arrowheads are identical, so tell them apart by the corners of the boxes: two rounded usages mean subsetting.`,result:`The electricGrid label gains :> externals, and a hollow-triangle line runs from electricGrid to externals.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u40-drag-edge-palette-new-subsetting.png`,alt:`The edge palette with New Subsetting among its tools.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u41-subsetting-drawn-arranged.png`,alt:`The subsetting line drawn from electricGrid to externals.`}]},{title:`Redefine crossesBoundary on electricGrid`,text:`Expand all. On <EXT-002> electricGrid, open ⋮ > New object > Attribute > CREATE. On its attribute1, open ⋮ > New object > Redefinition > CREATE. Set Redefined Feature to ExternalSystem > crossesBoundary, and Redefining Feature to context > electricGrid > attribute1. Click attribute1, click “+” next to Expression value, type "power to the vertiport chargers" with the double quotes and click UPDATE. Clear Declared Name and press Enter. Right-click electricGrid and choose Show/Hide > Show valued content. If you would rather not type the quotes: instead of “+”, open ⋮ on attribute1, choose New object > Literal String > CREATE, and type the words in Value with no quotes.`,why:`This is the same recipe as padCount, on a usage this time. electricGrid has crossesBoundary because its definition inherits it. The double quotes mark the value as text.`,result:`electricGrid’s compartment shows :>> crossesBoundary = "power to the vertiport chargers".`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u43-redefined-feature-dialog-ExternalSystem-crossesBoundary.png`,alt:`The Select an object dialog with crossesBoundary under ExternalSystem highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u46-create-expression-dialog-string-value.png`,alt:`The Create Expression dialog holding the quoted text.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u48-electricGrid-shows-redefined-crossesBoundary.png`,alt:`electricGrid showing the redefined crossesBoundary in its compartment.`}]},{title:`Remove the imports SysON added`,text:`Expand all and look at the top of L2 Specialization. Delete these four import rows, each with ⋮ > Delete > DELETE: context::electricGrid::*, ExternalSystem::*, HubVertiport::* and Vertiport::*. Keep import ScalarValues::*.`,why:`SysON adds an import when you pick a feature from another element. Your model does not need these four: inheritance already gives the access. ScalarValues stays because String and Natural come from it.`,result:`Only import ScalarValues::* is left at the top of the package.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u49-explorer-shows-imports-added-by-syson.png`,alt:`The Explorer showing five import rows.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u51-only-scalarvalues-import-left.png`,alt:`The Explorer with only import ScalarValues::* left.`}]},{title:`Arrange the diagram and read it`,text:`Click the empty canvas, then use Arrange all and Fit to screen. Zoom in to read the lines.`,why:`Compare it with the diagram in step 2 of this section. The positions differ, but the content is the same: follow each triangle to its general element, each diamond to its owner, and each defined-by line to its definition.`,result:`Four square-cornered definitions, three rounded usages and seven lines.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u52-final-model-arranged.png`,alt:`The final diagram: four part definitions, three part usages and seven lines.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u53-zoom-subsetting-and-typing-notation.png`,alt:`A close-up of the arrowheads on the subsetting and defined-by lines.`}]},{title:`Confirm the model is saved`,text:`SysON saves each change as you make it, so there is no Save button. Close the browser tab. Open SysON again, open AeroLink Week 8 Section 2 from the project list, and open view1. Click the two nameless attributes in the Explorer, then electricGrid.`,why:`Reopening proves that your work is in the model, not only on your screen. Check the values that are easiest to lose: the two redefinitions, their values and the short name.`,result:`All seven elements and seven lines are back. The attributes keep Redefines and Expression value; electricGrid keeps EXT-002, Subsets externals and Typed by Electric grid.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u56-reopened-diagram.png`,alt:`The reopened diagram with all elements and both compartment values.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u57-reopened-hub-padCount-redefines-value-4.png`,alt:`Details after reopening: Redefines padCount and Expression value 4.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u59-reopened-electricGrid-short-name-typed-by.png`,alt:`Details for electricGrid after reopening: short name, Subsets and Typed by.`}]},{title:`Inspect the completed model`,text:`Open the ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml and choose Expand all. Read the tree from top to bottom against the checklist below. Then click the warning triangle on the left edge to open the Validation panel.`,why:`The Explorer shows what the diagram does not draw. Check for: one import, of ScalarValues; four part definitions; a Redefinition under each nameless attribute; a [0,*] row under externals; context with its two parts.`,result:`The Validation panel reports 66 warnings on this correct model. Each one has been traced: none is a defect in the model, 62 are false alarms and 4 come from unfinished parts of SysON. The list below explains them. If your count differs, compare your model with these steps. The panel is separate from the OMG checker, which is the reference.`,warnings:s(2),imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u54-explorer-tree-expanded.png`,alt:`The fully expanded Explorer tree.`},{src:`syson/week8/s2-u62-validation-panel.png`,alt:`The Validation panel reporting 66 diagnostics.`}]},{title:`Optional: see the SysML text`,text:`Open the ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml again and choose Download. Open the downloaded file in any text editor and compare it with the SysML text in step 2 of this section.`,why:`You did not write this text: SysON generated it from your diagram and the values you entered. Nothing here needs editing.`,result:`The same definitions, usages, specializations and redefinitions as the listing. SysON adds a view block for its diagram and writes subsets externals in its short form, :> externals.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s2-u61-export-document-menu-download.png`,alt:`The document menu with Download.`}]}],after:[`What you built: two kinds of general definition, each with a specialized definition; one redefinition on a definition and one on a usage; and a usage that subsets a collection.`,`It is the same model as the listing in step 2. When the course team exported this model with SysON’s Download command and ran the course checker on the file, it passed. SysON adds a view block for its diagram.`,`For your team’s enterprise model: use the same pattern. Where several of your elements are one kind of thing, give them a general definition and specialize it. Where a specialized kind fixes a value, redefine the feature. Where a usage belongs to a collection, subset it.`,`Your SysON work is not marked and the app cannot see it. SysON’s export of other constructs (requirements, interfaces, viewpoints) still has known faults, so the course does not grade SysON exports.`]}},next:[{week:11,text:`Physical variants and configurations specialize and redefine the physical definitions.`}]},{id:`packages`,title:`The register: packages, short names and imports`,known:{week:1,text:`The Team Record: the register of IDs (ENT, STK, CON, NEED, REQ …) your team kept in Phase I.`},representation:`Each register section becomes a package. Each row keeps its Phase I ID as a short name, so the ID stays the stable reference across weeks. A package refers to another package’s elements by a qualified name (Context::'ENT-001') or brings them into scope with a private import.`,constructs:[{text:`package Name { … }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.5 Namespaces and Packages, pp.55–57`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`55–57`}},{text:`private import Context::*;  (an import always states its visibility)`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.5.3 Imports, pp.60–61`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`60–61`}},{text:`Qualified name Context::'AeroLink Urban Mobility'`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.5 Namespaces and Packages, p.56`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`56`}},{text:`One package per register section (Context, Stakeholders, Needs, Requirements …)`,kind:`pattern`,clause:`Course convention; the lab tool generates this layout`}],file:u(`learn_03_packages_imports`),focus:[{line:9,text:`The Stakeholders package imports what Context declares.`},{line:12,text:`So ENT-001 resolves here without a qualified name.`},{line:18,text:`Needs does not import Context, so it uses the qualified name.`}],figure:{id:`packages`,kind:`native`,caption:`Packages are drawn as folders with a tab. A private import is an arrow labeled «private import»* from the importing package to the imported one (the * marks “all members”). Course-simplified: package contents are listed as text.`},meaning:`The three packages are separate namespaces: two elements may share a name if they are in different packages. Stakeholders can refer to ENT-001 directly because of its import; Needs reaches the same element through a qualified name. Both references point to the same definition.`,relationships:[{text:`A package imports another package (private import Context::*).`,valid:!0,why:`Imported members become visible by name.`},{text:`An import written without private or public.`,valid:!1,why:`The OMG Pilot Implementation requires the visibility on every import.`},{text:`A name used in a package that neither declares nor imports it, without a qualified name.`,valid:!1,why:`The name does not resolve.`}],readings:[{text:`Two packages that both mention ENT-001 hold two copies of it.`,correct:!1,why:`There is one declaration; the other package only refers to it.`},{text:`private import makes the imported names visible inside this package only.`,correct:!0,why:`Private members are not re-exported to packages that import this one.`},{text:`<'NEED-001'> and 'Short, predictable wait to board' must both be written whenever you refer to the need.`,correct:!1,why:`Either the short name or the name is enough.`}],aerolink:[`ENT-001`,`STK-001`,`CON-001`,`NEED-001`],next:[{week:9,text:`Needs and requirements go into the Needs and Requirements packages created this week.`},{week:12,text:`The digital thread follows these qualified references across packages.`}]},{id:`stakeholders`,title:`Stakeholders and concerns`,known:{week:1,text:`The stakeholder register (STK rows) and each stakeholder’s concerns (CON rows).`},representation:`SysML v2 has no stakeholder definition keyword. The course writes each stakeholder row as a part definition with its STK ID. Each concern row becomes a concern usage with a subject (what the concern is about) and one or more stakeholder usages (whose concern it is), each defined by a stakeholder part definition.`,constructs:[{text:`concern <'CON-001'> Name { … }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.21.3 Concern Definitions and Usages, p.167`,cite:{reading:`R83`,pages:`167`}},{text:`subject name : Definition;  (first parameter)`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.21.2 Requirement Definition and Usage, pp.165–166`,cite:{reading:`R83`,pages:`165–166`}},{text:`stakeholder name : PartDefinition;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.21.2 Requirement Definition and Usage, pp.165–166`,cite:{reading:`R83`,pages:`165–166`}},{text:`Each stakeholder row as a part def named by its STK ID`,kind:`pattern`,clause:`Course convention (the specification requires only that a stakeholder be defined by a part definition)`}],file:u(`learn_04_stakeholders_concerns`),focus:[{line:7,text:`A stakeholder row as a part definition.`},{line:11,text:`The subject comes first: the concern is about the enterprise.`},{line:12,text:`Whose concern it is: a stakeholder usage defined by STK-001.`}],figure:{id:`concern`,kind:`native`,caption:`A concern usage is a rounded node «concern» with its subject in a subject compartment; each stakeholder is a rounded «stakeholder» node joined to its concern by a plain line, with a defined-by line to the part definition that defines it. SysON labels the stakeholder node «part»: a stakeholder is a part usage. The stakeholder definitions are part definitions. Course-simplified: the documentation compartment is omitted.`},meaning:`Passengers (STK-001) are concerned about predictable waiting time (CON-001), and Communities (STK-004) about take-off and landing noise (CON-012). Both concerns are about the AeroLink enterprise (ENT-001). Each concern is now a model element: later requirements can frame it, and a viewpoint can collect it.`,relationships:[{text:`A stakeholder usage is defined by a part definition.`,valid:!0,why:`Stakeholders are part usages.`},{text:`A stakeholder usage is defined by an attribute definition.`,valid:!1,why:`The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects it: an occurrence, item or part must be typed by occurrence definitions.`},{text:`A stakeholder declared before the subject.`,valid:!1,why:`The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects it: the subject must be the first parameter.`}],invalid:{file:u(`invalid_learn_stakeholder_def`),text:`A “stakeholder def” keyword does not exist. The OMG Pilot Implementation stops at line 4.`},readings:[{text:`stakeholder passengers : STK-001 creates the stakeholder Passengers.`,correct:!1,why:`The part definition STK-001 is the stakeholder type; the stakeholder usage says Passengers hold THIS concern.`},{text:`The subject of CON-001 is what the concern is about, not who holds it.`,correct:!0,why:`The subject is the enterprise; the holder is the stakeholder usage.`},{text:`A concern needs a doc comment to be valid.`,correct:!1,why:`The doc is good practice (here it carries a shortened form of the register text) but is not required.`}],aerolink:[`ENT-001`,`STK-001`,`STK-004`,`CON-001`,`CON-012`],next:[{week:9,text:`Each need frames the concerns modeled here.`}],diagram:{readings:[{text:`The rounded boxes are usages and the square-cornered boxes are definitions: the two concerns are usages, and the ENT and STK boxes are part definitions.`,correct:!0,why:`SysML v2 draws a definition with square corners and a usage with rounded corners. «concern» marks a concern usage; «part def» marks a part definition.`},{text:`The box passengers : 'STK-001' is the definition of the Passengers stakeholder.`,correct:!1,why:`It has rounded corners and is marked «stakeholder», so it is a usage: the stakeholder of CON-001, joined to the concern by a line. Its defined-by line points to the square-cornered part definition STK-001 Passengers, which defines it.`},{text:`The subject compartment shows what a concern is about: both concerns are about the AeroLink enterprise.`,correct:!0,why:`Both subject entries read enterprise : 'ENT-001'. Who holds each concern is in the stakeholders compartment.`},{text:`Communities hold the concern about predictable waiting time.`,correct:!1,why:`The line from CON-001 goes to the stakeholder passengers : 'STK-001'. Communities (STK-004) are joined to CON-012, Community noise.`}],mistake:{file:u(`invalid_learn_stakeholder_attribute_def`),figure:`concern`,text:`This diagram draws an intentionally invalid model. One element breaks a SysML v2 rule. Click the element where the OMG Pilot Implementation reports the error.`,accept:[{line:7,text:`Found it. The stakeholder passengers : 'STK-001' is defined by an attribute definition. The OMG Pilot Implementation reports: “An occurrence, item or part must be typed by occurrence definitions.” A stakeholder is a part usage, so STK-001 must be a part def.`},{line:4,text:`You found the cause. «attribute def» STK-001 is legal on its own, but the stakeholder passengers : 'STK-001' uses it, and a stakeholder must be defined by a part definition. The OMG Pilot Implementation reports the error on that stakeholder: “An occurrence, item or part must be typed by occurrence definitions.”`}]},build:{figure:`concern`,text:`Build CON-012 from the Week 1 register: Communities (STK-004) are concerned about take-off and landing noise. Choose the kind of each element, the subject and the stakeholder. The diagram redraws as you choose.`,slots:[{id:`con-kind`,label:`What kind of element is CON-012 'Community noise'?`,options:[{id:`concern`,text:`concern`,ok:!0,feedback:`A register CON row becomes a concern usage, drawn rounded and marked «concern».`},{id:`requirement`,text:`requirement`,ok:!1,feedback:`A requirement comes later: in section 6 a need frames the concern. The concern row itself is a concern.`},{id:`partdef`,text:`part def`,ok:!1,feedback:`A part definition is for a stakeholder or the enterprise, not for what they are concerned about.`}]},{id:`subject`,label:`Subject: what is the concern about?`,options:[{id:`ent`,text:`'ENT-001'`,ok:!0,feedback:`The concern is about the AeroLink enterprise, as CON-001 is.`},{id:`stk`,text:`'STK-004'`,ok:!1,feedback:`STK-004 holds the concern, so it is the stakeholder, the node joined to the concern by a line. The subject is what the concern is about.`}]},{id:`stakeholder`,label:`Stakeholder: whose concern is it?`,options:[{id:`stk`,text:`'STK-004'`,ok:!0,feedback:`Communities hold this concern.`},{id:`ent`,text:`'ENT-001'`,ok:!1,feedback:`The enterprise is what the concern is about, not who holds it.`}]},{id:`stk-kind`,label:`What kind of element is STK-004 Communities?`,options:[{id:`partdef`,text:`part def`,ok:!0,feedback:`Course pattern: each stakeholder row becomes a part definition named by its STK ID, and a stakeholder must be defined by a part definition.`},{id:`attrdef`,text:`attribute def`,ok:!1,feedback:`The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects a stakeholder defined by an attribute definition: the mistake in step 4.`},{id:`concern`,text:`concern`,ok:!1,feedback:`A concern is what a stakeholder cares about. The stakeholder itself is defined by a part definition.`}]}],done:`Your diagram matches the checked model: CON-012 is a concern about the enterprise, held by Communities, and STK-004 is a part definition.`,lines:[8,14,15,16,17,18]},elements:[{at:9,name:`«concern» <'CON-001'> 'Predictable waiting time'`,means:`A concern usage: what Passengers care about, predictable waiting time, recorded as a model element with the Week 1 register ID CON-001.`,why:`It is drawn rounded because it is a usage. Its subject compartment says what the concern is about, and the line below it leads to the stakeholder who holds it.`},{at:14,name:`«concern» <'CON-012'> 'Community noise'`,means:`A concern usage: what Communities care about, take-off and landing noise, with the Week 1 register ID CON-012.`,why:`Like CON-001 it is about the enterprise (its subject compartment), and the line below it leads to its stakeholder, Communities.`},{at:11,name:`subject enterprise : 'ENT-001' of CON-001`,means:`The subject: CON-001 is about the AeroLink enterprise.`,why:`Every concern has exactly one subject, the thing it is about, and the OMG Pilot Implementation requires it to come first. It is a parameter of the concern that refers to ENT-001, which is why it is written in the concern's subject compartment instead of being drawn as a line to the AeroLink box.`},{at:16,name:`subject enterprise : 'ENT-001' of CON-012`,means:`The subject: CON-012 is also about the AeroLink enterprise.`,why:`Two concerns can share a subject. Each concern has its own subject parameter, and both refer to the same part definition, ENT-001.`},{at:12,name:`«stakeholder» passengers : 'STK-001'`,means:`The stakeholder of CON-001: Passengers hold this concern.`,why:`A stakeholder is a part usage owned by the concern as a parameter, so the specification draws it as a «stakeholder» node joined to its concern by a plain line. Its defined-by line points to the part definition STK-001, which says what kind of thing the stakeholder is. SysON labels this node «part».`},{at:17,name:`«stakeholder» communities : 'STK-004'`,means:`The stakeholder of CON-012: Communities hold this concern.`,why:`Joined to CON-012 by the plain line, and defined by the part definition STK-004 through its defined-by line.`},{at:7,name:`«part def» <'STK-001'> Passengers`,means:`The part definition for the Week 1 stakeholder row STK-001, Passengers.`,why:`It is square-cornered because it is a definition, a reusable type. The stakeholder passengers is defined by it, and any other concern Passengers hold would be defined by the same part definition.`},{at:8,name:`«part def» <'STK-004'> Communities`,means:`The part definition for the Week 1 stakeholder row STK-004, Communities.`,why:`The stakeholder communities of CON-012 is defined by it.`},{at:6,name:`«part def» <'ENT-001'> 'AeroLink Urban Mobility'`,means:`The part definition for the enterprise itself, ENT-001.`,why:`Both concerns are about it: each subject compartment names 'ENT-001'. No line is drawn to it, because a subject is shown in its concern's subject compartment; the course does not add a line that the model does not contain.`}],tool:{title:`Now build it in SysON`,intro:`You have read and built the diagram here. Now build the same model in SysON, the graphical SysML v2 modeling tool the course selected for Week 8: three part definitions and the two concerns CON-001 and CON-012, each with its subject and stakeholder, by clicking and filling in fields only. You do not type any SysML. Each step says what to do, why, and what your screen should look like. Open SysON at the address your instructor gives you. This tutorial is practice in the tool. It is not your team’s enterprise model and it is not submitted: your enterprise model is the .sysml file the lab tools draft and the course checker checks.`,steps:[{title:`Create the project AeroLink Week 8`,text:`On the SysON home page, click the SysMLv2 card under Create a new project. Type the name AeroLink Week 8 and click CREATE PROJECT.`,why:`The project holds this section’s model. Each Week 8 section has its own SysON project, so that each model can be exported and checked on its own. Give it this exact name; your instructor can then find it.`,result:`The new-project form shows the name AeroLink Week 8 and the template SysMLv2. The version of SysON is printed at the bottom of the page.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t01-home-projects.png`,alt:`The SysON home page: template cards across the top, including SysMLv2, and the Existing Projects list below.`},{src:`syson/week8/t02-new-project-form.png`,alt:`The Create a new project form with the name AeroLink Week 8 typed in, the SysMLv2 template chosen, and the CREATE PROJECT button.`}]},{title:`Open the General View and name the package`,text:`Click the view1 link to open the diagram. In the Explorer on the left, expand SysMLv2.sysml and click Package1. In Details on the right, set Declared Name to L4 Stakeholders and concerns and press Enter.`,why:`A package is a named container for model elements. This one matches the package of the checked listing in step 2, so your model and the course listing use the same names.`,result:`An empty General View with the hint Add your first element; Details shows the new name and the qualified name.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t03-empty-general-view.png`,alt:`The empty General View canvas showing the hint Add your first element, with the Explorer on the left and Details on the right.`},{src:`syson/week8/t04-rename-root-package.png`,alt:`Details for the package: Declared Name L4 Stakeholders and concerns, with its qualified name below.`}]},{title:`Create the three part definitions`,text:`Right-click an empty spot on the canvas, open Structure and click New Part Definition. In Details set Declared Name to AeroLink Urban Mobility and Declared Short Name to ENT-001, pressing Enter after each. Repeat for Passengers with short name STK-001, and Communities with short name STK-004.`,why:`A part definition is a reusable type, drawn square-cornered. ENT-001 is the enterprise row of your Phase I baseline; STK-001 and STK-004 are two rows of the Week 1 stakeholder register. The short name keeps the register ID on the element.`,result:`Three square-cornered «part def» nodes, each showing its short name in angle brackets, for example <ENT-001> AeroLink Urban Mobility.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t05-palette-structure-new-part-definition.png`,alt:`The canvas right-click palette with the Structure section open and New Part Definition highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/t06-partdef-ENT-001.png`,alt:`A «part def» node <ENT-001> AeroLink Urban Mobility on the canvas, with Declared Name and Declared Short Name filled in under Details.`},{src:`syson/week8/t08-partdef-STK-004.png`,alt:`Three «part def» nodes on the canvas: ENT-001 AeroLink Urban Mobility, STK-001 Passengers and STK-004 Communities.`}]},{title:`Create the concern CON-001`,text:`Right-click an empty spot on the canvas, open Requirements and click New Concern. In Details set Declared Name to Predictable waiting time and Declared Short Name to CON-001. In Documentation type: Passengers judge the service by how long they wait between confirming a ride and boarding. Press Tab to leave the field.`,why:`A concern usage records what a stakeholder cares about. CON-001 is a row of the Week 1 concern register, so the name, ID and description come from there. Details also shows Typed by ConcernCheck: that is a SysON default, it is not saved in your model, and you can ignore it.`,result:`A rounded «concern» node <CON-001> Predictable waiting time. Details shows Req Id CON-001, filled in from the short name.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t09-palette-requirements-new-concern.png`,alt:`The canvas right-click palette with the Requirements section open and New Concern highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/t10-concern-CON-001-properties.png`,alt:`A rounded «concern» node CON-001 Predictable waiting time, with its name, short name and documentation set under Details.`}]},{title:`Give the concern its subject`,text:`Right-click the concern node, open Structure and click New Subject. Choose Select an existing Element as subject, select AeroLink Urban Mobility in the tree and click CONFIRM. SysON names the new subject subject: rename it enterprise in Details.`,why:`The subject is what the concern is about: here, the AeroLink enterprise. It is a parameter of the concern that refers to ENT-001, so SysON writes it in the concern node, in a subject compartment, and draws no line to the AeroLink box.`,result:`The concern gains a subject compartment reading ref enterprise : AeroLink Urban Mobility. Details shows Typed by AeroLink Urban Mobility. SysON writes ref in the diagram; in SysML text the same element is written with the keyword subject.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t12-subject-dialog-select-ENT-001.png`,alt:`The New Subject dialog with Select an existing Element chosen and AeroLink Urban Mobility selected; the footer says it will create a subject specialized with AeroLink Urban Mobility.`},{src:`syson/week8/t13-subject-renamed-enterprise.png`,alt:`The concern node with a subject compartment reading ref enterprise : AeroLink Urban Mobility, and Details showing the name enterprise.`}]},{title:`Add the stakeholder passengers`,text:`Right-click the concern, open Structure and click New Stakeholder. Choose Create a new stakeholder and click CONFIRM. In Details set Declared Name to passengers, click in Typed by, type Passengers and pick the one option offered.`,why:`The stakeholder is who holds the concern: Passengers, row STK-001. SysON’s list of existing elements for a stakeholder does not show part definitions, so you create a new stakeholder and then type it by Passengers; the result is the same model element. The new node may land on top of others; the next step tidies it.`,result:`A rounded node labeled «part» passengers : Passengers, joined to the concern by a plain line. SysON labels a stakeholder «part» because a stakeholder is a part usage; the course diagram uses the specification’s label «stakeholder».`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t14-stakeholder-dialog-create-new.png`,alt:`The New Stakeholder dialog with Create a new stakeholder chosen; the list of existing elements shows no part definitions.`},{src:`syson/week8/t15-stakeholder-typed-by-autocomplete.png`,alt:`Details for the new stakeholder named passengers, with Passengers typed into Typed by and a single option, Passengers, offered. The new node overlaps other nodes.`},{src:`syson/week8/t16-stakeholder-passengers-typed.png`,alt:`The stakeholder node now reads passengers : Passengers and Details shows Passengers in Typed by.`}]},{title:`Tidy the diagram`,text:`Click an empty spot on the canvas so nothing is selected. Click Arrange all in the toolbar, then Fit to screen.`,why:`Arrange all only moves nodes on the diagram; it does not change the model. If a node is still selected, Fit to screen zooms into that node instead of the whole diagram.`,result:`CON-001 with its subject compartment, joined by a line to «part» passengers : Passengers, and the three part definitions beside it.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t17-arrange-all-after-CON-001.png`,alt:`The arranged diagram: the concern CON-001 with its subject compartment, a plain line down to the «part» node passengers : Passengers, and the three part definitions.`}]},{title:`Add CON-012 the same way`,text:`Repeat steps 4 to 7 for the second concern: name Community noise, short name CON-012, Documentation: Communities near vertiports are concerned about take-off and landing noise. Its subject is AeroLink Urban Mobility, renamed enterprise; its stakeholder is communities, typed by Communities.`,why:`CON-012 is the Week 1 concern of the Communities stakeholder (STK-004). It has the same subject as CON-001: two concerns can be about the same enterprise.`,result:`The full model: three part definitions and two concerns, each with a subject compartment and a line to its stakeholder node. Compare it with the diagram you built earlier in this section: the same elements, joined the same way.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t24-arrange-all-full-model.png`,alt:`The full model in SysON: part definitions ENT-001, STK-001 and STK-004; concerns CON-001 and CON-012, each with a subject compartment ref enterprise : AeroLink Urban Mobility and a line to its «part» stakeholder node, passengers : Passengers and communities : Communities.`}]},{title:`Confirm your work is saved`,text:`SysON has no Save button: every change is stored on the server as you make it (text fields when you press Enter or Tab). To confirm, close the browser tab, open the SysON home page again and click AeroLink Week 8 under Existing Projects. In the Explorer, open the menu ⋮ beside SysMLv2.sysml and choose Expand all, then click view1.`,why:`Reopening the project from the home page is the only way to see what the server kept. If something is missing after reopening, it was not stored: usually a field left without pressing Enter or Tab.`,result:`The project is listed on the home page, and after reopening the diagram and the Explorer tree show every element. Click CON-001 in the Explorer: its name, short name and documentation are still set.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t26-reopen-home-project-list.png`,alt:`The SysON home page after reopening the browser, with AeroLink Week 8 at the top of Existing Projects.`},{src:`syson/week8/t27-reopened-diagram-and-explorer.png`,alt:`The reopened project: the expanded Explorer tree lists the package, view1, the three part definitions and both concerns with their Documentation, enterprise and stakeholder children; the diagram shows all the nodes.`},{src:`syson/week8/t28-reopened-CON-001-details.png`,alt:`Details for CON-001 after reopening: name, short name, Req Id and documentation are all still set.`}]},{title:`Inspect the completed model`,text:`Read the expanded Explorer tree from top to bottom against the checklist below. Then click the warning triangle on the left edge to open the Validation panel.`,why:`The Explorer shows every element in the model, including ones the diagram does not draw. Check for: three part definitions with the short names ENT-001, STK-001 and STK-004; two concerns CON-001 and CON-012, each with Documentation, a subject named enterprise and one stakeholder; each subject and stakeholder with a FeatureTyping child, which is its defined-by link.`,result:`The Validation panel reports 29 warnings on this correct model. Each one has been traced: none is a defect in the model, 17 are false alarms and 12 come from how SysON stores the model. The list below explains them. If your count differs, compare your model with these steps. The panel is separate from the OMG checker, which is the reference.`,warnings:s(4),imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/t25-explorer-tree-expanded.png`,alt:`The fully expanded Explorer tree: each concern lists Documentation, enterprise and its stakeholder, each with a FeatureTyping child.`},{src:`syson/week8/t30-validation-panel.png`,alt:`The Validation panel opened from the warning triangle on the left edge, reporting Warning, 29 diagnostics.`}]}],after:[`What you built: the stakeholder-and-concern layer of the AeroLink baseline. Two Week 1 concerns (CON-001, CON-012), each about the enterprise ENT-001 and held by a stakeholder from the register (STK-001, STK-004).`,`It is the same model as the listing in step 2. When the course team exported this model with SysON’s Download command and ran the course checker on the file, it passed; SysON adds a view block for its diagram and leaves the rest as written.`,`On your own: open the Explorer menu ⋮ on SysMLv2.sysml and choose Download to see the SysML text of your model. Compare it with the listing.`,`Your SysON work is not marked and the app cannot see it. SysON’s export of other constructs (requirements, interfaces, viewpoints) still has known faults, so the course does not grade SysON exports.`]}}},{id:`relationships`,title:`Ratings and stakeholder relationships: metadata and dependencies`,known:{week:1,text:`The interest and influence ratings, and the relationship rows (REL) between stakeholders.`},representation:`The ratings become metadata: a metadata definition declares the fields, and an @ annotation attaches values to a stakeholder definition. A relationship row becomes a dependency from the dependent element (client) to the element it depends on (supplier). A course metadata definition with a short name can tag the dependency with a #keyword.`,constructs:[{text:`metadata def Name { attribute … }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.27.2 Metadata Definitions and Usages, p.191`,cite:{reading:`R69`,pages:`191`}},{text:`@Name { field = value; }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.27.1 Metadata Overview, pp.188–190`,cite:{reading:`R69`,pages:`188–190`}},{text:`dependency <'REL-001'> from A to B;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.3 Dependencies, pp.50–51`,cite:{reading:`R80`,pages:`50–51`}},{text:`#regulatedBy  (a user-defined keyword from a metadata def short name)`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.27.4 User-Defined Keywords, p.193`,cite:{reading:`R69`,pages:`193`}},{text:`StakeholderAssessment, regulatedBy`,kind:`pattern`,clause:`Course metadata definitions (not in any standard library)`}],file:u(`learn_05_metadata_dependency`),focus:[{line:14,text:`The FAA row’s ratings attached as metadata.`},{line:20,text:`The register says the FAA regulates the manufacturer: the manufacturer depends on the FAA, so it is the client after from.`}],figure:{id:`dependency`,kind:`native`,caption:`A dependency is a dashed line with an open arrowhead from the client to the supplier. Course-simplified: the user-defined keyword is shown as a label on the edge, and the ratings are shown in the text only.`},meaning:`The aircraft manufacturer depends on the FAA: a change to the FAA may result in a change to the manufacturer. The #regulatedBy keyword records which Phase I relationship type the dependency stands for. The ratings are data about the register rows; they do not change what the model says about the system.`,relationships:[{text:`A dependency from the dependent element to the element it depends on.`,valid:!0,why:`The specification: a change to the supplier may result in a change to the client.`},{text:`The same dependency written from the FAA to the manufacturer.`,valid:!0,why:`The code is still valid, but it now says the FAA depends on the manufacturer. No checker can catch a reversed direction: only the register can.`},{text:`A #keyword that names something other than a metadata definition.`,valid:!1,why:`A user-defined keyword must name a metadata definition.`}],readings:[{text:`from STK-011 to STK-002 reads “the manufacturer depends on the FAA”.`,correct:!0,why:`The client follows from, the supplier follows to.`},{text:`#regulatedBy is a SysML v2 keyword.`,correct:!1,why:`regulatedBy is the short name of a course metadata definition; # makes it usable as a keyword.`},{text:`Because the listing passes the OMG Pilot Implementation, the direction matches the register.`,correct:!1,why:`A PASS shows the text parsed and resolved, not that it says what the register says.`}],aerolink:[`STK-002`,`STK-011`,`REL-001`],next:[{week:12,text:`Trace relationships in the digital thread use the same client and supplier reading.`}]},{id:`requirements`,title:`Needs and requirements`,known:{week:2,text:`Stakeholder needs (NEED) and the requirements derived from them (REQ), with their measures.`},representation:`A need becomes a requirement usage that frames the concerns it answers, with the enterprise as subject. A requirement adds a required constraint over a feature of the subject. A satisfy relationship states that a usage meets a requirement.`,constructs:[{text:`requirement <'NEED-001'> Name { subject …; frame 'CON-001'; }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.21.2 Requirement Definition and Usage, pp.165–167`,cite:{reading:`R83`,pages:`165–167`}},{text:`require constraint { … }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.21.2 Requirement Definition and Usage, p.166`,cite:{reading:`R83`,pages:`166`}},{text:`satisfy 'REQ-STK-001' by aeroLink;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.21.4 Satisfy Requirement Usages, p.168`,cite:{reading:`R83`,pages:`168`}},{text:`DurationValue, min`,kind:`library`,clause:`ISQ and SI libraries (imported)`},{text:`A need written as a requirement usage that frames concerns`,kind:`pattern`,clause:`Course convention (SysML v2 has no separate “need” construct)`}],file:u(`learn_06_needs_requirements`),focus:[{line:19,text:`The need frames the concern CON-001.`},{line:25,text:`The requirement states its measure as a required constraint.`},{line:28,text:`The usage aeroLink is asserted to satisfy the requirement.`}],figure:{id:`requirement`,kind:`native`,caption:`A «frame» edge runs from the requirement to the concern it frames; the «satisfy» shorthand runs from the satisfying usage to the requirement; the required constraint hangs from the requirement by a filled diamond. Course-simplified: subject and stakeholder compartments are omitted, and the constraint node shows its expression where a name would go.`},meaning:`NEED-001 addresses the passengers’ concern CON-001. REQ-STK-001 requires that the enterprise’s 90th-percentile wait be at most 15 minutes, and the model asserts that aeroLink satisfies it. Whether it really does is a verification question for Week 12.`,relationships:[{text:`A requirement frames a concern usage.`,valid:!0,why:`frame names a concern.`},{text:`A requirement frames a stakeholder part definition.`,valid:!1,why:`The OMG Pilot Implementation cannot resolve it as a concern feature.`},{text:`satisfy … by a part definition.`,valid:!1,why:`The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects it: satisfy … by needs a feature (a usage).`}],invalid:{file:u(`invalid_learn_frame_unresolved`),text:`A need that frames a concern the model does not contain. The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects line 12.`},readings:[{text:`frame CON-001 means NEED-001 is about the concern CON-001.`,correct:!1,why:`The subject says what the need is about (the enterprise); frame says which concern the need answers.`},{text:`satisfy REQ-STK-001 by aeroLink proves that AeroLink meets the requirement.`,correct:!1,why:`It is an assertion in the model; verification provides the evidence.`},{text:`The required constraint is the measurable part of the requirement.`,correct:!0,why:`It is a Boolean expression over features of the subject.`}],aerolink:[`ENT-001`,`STK-001`,`CON-001`,`NEED-001`,`REQ-STK-001`],next:[{week:9,text:`All needs and requirements are modeled, with derivation from needs.`},{week:12,text:`Verification cases check what the satisfy relationships assert.`}]},{id:`scenarios`,title:`ConOps scenarios: use cases`,known:{week:2,text:`The ConOps scenarios (SCN rows): who does what with the enterprise, and what success looks like.`},representation:`A scenario becomes a use case with the enterprise as subject, the participating stakeholders as actors and the success condition as its objective.`,constructs:[{text:`use case <'SCN-001'> Name { … }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.25 Use Cases, p.177`,cite:{reading:`R76`,pages:`100–107`}},{text:`subject …; actor name : PartDefinition;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.25.1 Use Cases Overview, p.177`,cite:{reading:`R76`,pages:`100–107`}},{text:`objective { doc /* … */ }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.22 Cases, p.169`,cite:{reading:`R76`,pages:`100–107`}}],file:u(`learn_07_use_case`),focus:[{line:8,text:`The subject: the enterprise performs the use case.`},{line:9,text:`The actor: passengers take part from outside.`},{line:10,text:`The objective records the success condition.`}],figure:{id:`use-case`,kind:`native`,caption:`General view of the listing. A use case usage is a rounded node «use case» with subject and objective compartments; the actor is a stick figure outside it, joined by a plain line. Course-simplified: the use case’s doc is not drawn, and the definition of the subject is read from its row. SysON draws the objective as a separate «requirement» box.`},meaning:`Passengers take part in the ride-request use case of the AeroLink enterprise, and the use case succeeds when a booking is confirmed with an aircraft and a boarding time. The steps of the scenario are added as actions in Week 10.`,relationships:[{text:`An actor defined by a stakeholder part definition.`,valid:!0,why:`Actors are part usages.`},{text:`An actor declared before the subject.`,valid:!1,why:`The subject must be the first parameter.`},{text:`A use case with two subjects.`,valid:!1,why:`A use case has one subject. Someone who takes part from outside is an actor.`}],invalid:{file:u(`invalid_learn_two_subjects`),text:`A use case with two subjects. The OMG Pilot Implementation rejects line 7.`},readings:[{text:`The actor of SCN-001 is part of the AeroLink enterprise.`,correct:!1,why:`An actor is external to the subject.`},{text:`The objective states when the use case has achieved its purpose.`,correct:!0,why:`It is a requirement the use case is meant to satisfy.`}],aerolink:[`ENT-001`,`STK-001`,`SCN-001`],diagram:{readings:[{text:`The subject compartment says the use case is about the AeroLink enterprise.`,correct:!0,why:`The subject is what the use case is about. The enterprise performs it, so it sits inside the use case node as a compartment entry.`},{text:`The stick figure is drawn outside the use case because passengers are part of the enterprise.`,correct:!1,why:`It is the other way round. An actor is external to the subject, so the stick figure stands outside and joins the use case by a plain line.`},{text:`The objective compartment states when the use case has achieved its purpose.`,correct:!0,why:`It holds the success condition from the scenario: a confirmed booking with an aircraft and a boarding time.`},{text:`The plain line between the stick figure and the use case is a defined-by line.`,correct:!1,why:`A defined-by line has a triangle and two dots, like the one down to Passengers. The plain line says this actor takes part in this use case.`}],mistake:{file:u(`invalid_learn_two_subjects`),figure:`use-case`,text:`This diagram draws an intentionally invalid model. One element breaks a SysML v2 rule. Click the element where the OMG Pilot Implementation reports the error.`,accept:[{line:7,text:`Found it. A use case has one subject, and passengers is a second one. The OMG Pilot Implementation reports: “Only one subject is allowed.” Passengers take part from outside, so they should be an actor, drawn as a stick figure.`},{line:6,text:`Close: enterprise is the correct subject, and the error is on the row after it. The OMG Pilot Implementation reports on passengers: “Only one subject is allowed.” Passengers should be an actor.`}],miss:`Look at the subject compartment. How many rows should it have?`},build:{figure:`use-case`,text:`Finish the diagram of SCN-001: say what the use case is about, who takes part from outside, and when it has succeeded. The diagram redraws as you choose.`,slots:[{id:`subject`,label:`The enterprise performs this use case. The subject compartment reads:`,options:[{id:`enterprise`,text:`enterprise : 'ENT-001'`,ok:!0,feedback:`The subject is what the use case is about, and here the enterprise performs it.`},{id:`passengers`,text:`passengers : 'STK-001'`,ok:!1,feedback:`Passengers use the enterprise; they are not what the use case is about. They take part from outside as an actor.`}]},{id:`actor`,label:`The stick figure outside the use case is:`,options:[{id:`passengers`,text:`passengers : 'STK-001'`,ok:!0,feedback:`An actor plays a role from outside the subject, and passengers do.`},{id:`enterprise`,text:`enterprise : 'ENT-001'`,ok:!1,feedback:`The enterprise is the subject, and an actor is external to the subject.`}]},{id:`actor-def`,label:`The stick figure’s defined-by line ends at:`,options:[{id:`stk`,text:`'STK-001'`,ok:!0,feedback:`An actor is a part usage, so it is defined by a part definition: the stakeholder Passengers.`},{id:`ent`,text:`'ENT-001'`,ok:!1,feedback:`That definition is the enterprise. The actor is the stakeholder group, which has its own definition.`},{id:`scn`,text:`'SCN-001'`,ok:!1,feedback:`SCN-001 is the use case itself. The actor takes part in it, and is not defined by it.`}]},{id:`objective`,label:`The objective compartment holds:`,options:[{id:`success`,text:`Success: a confirmed booking`,ok:!0,feedback:`The objective records the success condition of the scenario.`},{id:`step`,text:`Step 1: the passenger opens the app`,ok:!1,feedback:`That is a step of the scenario. Steps become actions in Week 10; the objective says when the use case has succeeded.`},{id:`need`,text:`Passengers need a short wait`,ok:!1,feedback:`That is a stakeholder need, and needs were section 6. The objective is the success of this use case.`}]}],done:`Your diagram matches the checked model: the enterprise is the one subject, passengers are an actor defined by 'STK-001', and the objective records the success condition.`,lines:[8,9,11]},elements:[{at:6,name:`«use case» <'SCN-001'> 'Passenger Ride Request'`,means:`The Week 2 ConOps scenario SCN-001, as a use case.`,why:`It is rounded because it is a usage. Its Documentation holds the scenario summary, which the diagram does not draw.`},{at:8,name:`Subject enterprise : 'ENT-001'`,means:`The use case is about the AeroLink enterprise, which performs it.`,why:`A use case has one subject, and the subject is its first parameter. The row itself names the definition, so no line is drawn.`},{at:9,on:`stick figure`,name:`Stick figure passengers : 'STK-001'`,means:`The actor: passengers take part from outside the enterprise.`,why:`An actor is a part usage external to the subject, so it stands outside the use case.`},{at:9,on:`plain line`,name:`Plain line from the actor to the use case`,means:`This actor takes part in this use case.`,why:`It has no triangle and no diamond, because it says neither “defined by” nor “owned by”. It joins a participant to the use case.`},{at:9,on:`defined`,name:`Defined-by line from passengers to 'STK-001'`,means:`The actor is defined by the stakeholder definition Passengers.`,why:`An actor is a part usage, so a part definition defines it: the same defined-by line as section 1.`},{at:10,name:`Objective compartment`,means:`The use case succeeds when a booking is confirmed with an aircraft and a boarding time.`,why:`The objective is a requirement the use case is meant to satisfy, and its doc carries the success condition.`},{at:5,name:`«part def» <'STK-001'> Passengers`,means:`The Week 1 stakeholder, as a part definition.`,why:`It defines the actor, and it is square-cornered because it is a definition.`},{at:4,name:`«part def» <'ENT-001'> 'AeroLink Urban Mobility'`,means:`The enterprise, as a part definition.`,why:`The subject row names it. It is the same definition as in sections 1 to 6.`}],tool:{title:`Now build it in SysON`,intro:`You have read and built the diagram here. Now build the same model in SysON, the graphical SysML v2 modeling tool the course selected for Week 8: two part definitions and one use case with its subject, actor and objective, by clicking and filling in fields only. You do not type any SysML. Each step says what to do, why, and what your screen should look like. Open SysON at the address your instructor gives you. This tutorial is practice in the tool. It is not your team’s enterprise model and it is not submitted: your enterprise model is the .sysml file the lab tools draft and the course checker checks.`,steps:[{title:`Create the project AeroLink Week 8 Section 7`,text:`On the SysON home page, click the SysMLv2 card under Create a new project. Type the name AeroLink Week 8 Section 7 and click CREATE PROJECT. Then click Open view1.`,why:`Each Week 8 section has its own SysON project, so that each model can be exported and checked on its own. Give it this exact name; your instructor can then find it.`,result:`An empty General View canvas named view1. In the Explorer on the left, SysMLv2.sysml holds one package, Package1.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u01-new-project-form-sysmlv2-template.png`,alt:`The Create a new project form with the name AeroLink Week 8 Section 7 typed in.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u02-empty-general-view-view1.png`,alt:`The empty view1 canvas, with Package1 in the Explorer.`}]},{title:`Rename the package`,text:`Click Package1 in the Explorer. In Details on the right, set Declared Name to L7 Use cases and press Enter.`,why:`A package holds the whole model. The name in the Explorer and in the exported file comes from this field.`,result:`The Explorer and Details both show L7 Use cases.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u03-rename-root-package.png`,alt:`Details and the Explorer showing the package renamed to L7 Use cases.`}]},{title:`Create the two part definitions`,text:`Right-click the empty canvas and choose Structure > New Part Definition. In Details set Declared Name to AeroLink Urban Mobility and Declared Short Name to ENT-001, pressing Enter after each. Repeat for Passengers with short name STK-001. Then click the empty canvas and use Arrange all and Fit to screen.`,why:`The use case refers to these two definitions. They come first because the subject and actor dialogs in the next steps list existing elements to choose from.`,result:`Two square-cornered «part def» boxes, one labeled <ENT-001> AeroLink Urban Mobility and one <STK-001> Passengers.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u04-palette-structure-new-part-definition.png`,alt:`The canvas palette with Structure > New Part Definition highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u07-arrange-all-two-part-defs.png`,alt:`Two square-cornered part definition boxes after Arrange all.`}]},{title:`Create the use case`,text:`Right-click the empty canvas and choose Analysis > New Use Case. In Details set Declared Name to Passenger Ride Request and Declared Short Name to SCN-001, pressing Enter after each. In Documentation type: A passenger requests a ride and receives a confirmed booking with an assigned aircraft and boarding time. Click away from the field. Then use Arrange all and Fit to screen.`,why:`This is the ConOps scenario from Week 2. Typed by shows UseCase, which you did not set: SysON fills it in, so leave it. The Documentation text does not appear on the diagram; you read it in Details.`,result:`A rounded «use case» node with a small oval icon. SysON’s use case is a rounded box, not the classic ellipse.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u08-palette-analysis-new-use-case.png`,alt:`The canvas palette with Analysis > New Use Case highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u09-use-case-SCN-001-name-short-name-doc.png`,alt:`Details for the use case with its name, short name and Documentation filled in.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u10-use-case-arranged.png`,alt:`The rounded use case node after Arrange all.`}]},{title:`Add the subject`,text:`Right-click the header of the use case, near its name, and choose Structure > New Subject. In the dialog choose Select an existing Element as subject, click AeroLink Urban Mobility and click CONFIRM. In Details change Declared Name from subject to enterprise and press Enter.`,why:`The subject is what the use case is about: the enterprise performs it. You choose the definition from a list, so nothing is typed except the name. A use case has one subject, so once it has one, New Subject leaves this menu.`,result:`A subject compartment in the use case. It reads ref enterprise : AeroLink Urban Mobility. Details shows Typed by AeroLink Urban Mobility.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u11-use-case-palette-structure-new-subject.png`,alt:`The use case palette with Structure > New Subject highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u13-subject-dialog-select-ENT-001.png`,alt:`The subject dialog with AeroLink Urban Mobility selected.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u15-subject-named-enterprise.png`,alt:`Details showing the subject named enterprise, typed by AeroLink Urban Mobility.`}]},{title:`Add the actor`,text:`Use Arrange all and Fit to screen. Right-click the header of the use case and choose Structure > New Actor. Choose Select an existing Element as actor, click Passengers and click CONFIRM. In Details change Declared Name from actor1 to passengers and press Enter. Then use Arrange all and Fit to screen.`,why:`An actor takes part from outside the subject, so it is drawn outside the use case. Passengers are the actor here, not a second subject: this is the mistake from step 4.`,result:`A stick figure labeled passengers : Passengers, joined to the use case by a plain line. There is no defined-by line in SysON; the label and Typed by carry the definition.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u16-use-case-palette-structure-new-actor.png`,alt:`The use case palette with New Actor highlighted and New Subject no longer listed.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u17-actor-dialog-select-STK-001.png`,alt:`The actor dialog with Passengers selected.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u20-subject-compartment-actor-stick-figure-arranged.png`,alt:`The arranged diagram with a subject compartment and a stick figure joined by a plain line.`}]},{title:`Add the objective`,text:`Right-click the header of the use case, not the subject compartment, and choose Requirements > New Objective. Choose Create a new objective and click CONFIRM. Keep the name SysON gives it. In Documentation type: Success: a confirmed booking with an assigned aircraft and a boarding time. Click away from the field. Then use Arrange all and Fit to screen.`,why:`The objective is the success condition from the scenario. A right-click on the subject compartment opens a different menu with no Requirements section, so aim for the header. The listing’s objective has no name and SysON’s has a default one. The course keeps the default: removing it takes extra steps, and stopping halfway breaks the export.`,result:`A rounded box labeled «requirement», named Passenger Ride Request's objective, joined to the use case by a line with a filled diamond. Nothing on it says objective: the diamond line from the use case is what makes it the objective, and the course figure shows it as a compartment instead.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u21-use-case-palette-requirements-new-objective.png`,alt:`The use case palette with Requirements > New Objective highlighted.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u23-objective-dialog-create-new.png`,alt:`The objective dialog with Create a new objective selected.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u25-objective-documentation.png`,alt:`Details for the objective with its Documentation filled in.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u26-arranged-with-objective.png`,alt:`The final diagram with the use case, the stick figure and the objective box.`}]},{title:`Read the diagram and the Explorer`,text:`Compare your diagram with the one in step 2 of this section. Then open the ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml and choose Expand all.`,why:`The two pictures draw the same model differently. The course figure follows the specification’s compartments, and SysON draws the actor and the objective as their own nodes. The Explorer shows what the diagram leaves out, such as each Documentation entry.`,result:`In the Explorer the use case holds Documentation, enterprise, passengers and the objective, and the objective holds its own Documentation.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u27-explorer-tree-expanded.png`,alt:`The expanded Explorer tree under the use case.`}]},{title:`See why the diagram alone is not proof`,text:`Read this step; there is nothing to do. The course team forced a second subject into a use case by dragging one in the Explorer from another use case.`,why:`SysON removes New Subject once a use case has one, but this drag got past that. The diagram then showed only the first subject, and only the Explorer showed both. The OMG checker rejected the export with “Only one subject is allowed.”`,result:`The first picture shows the diagram with one subject. The second shows the Explorer of the same model with two. So check the Explorer as well as the diagram.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-m02-diagram-shows-only-first-subject.png`,alt:`A use case on the diagram showing only the subject enterprise, although the model has two subjects.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-m03-explorer-two-subjects-under-use-case.png`,alt:`The Explorer of the same model with two subjects under the use case.`}]},{title:`Confirm the model is saved`,text:`SysON saves each change as you make it, so there is no Save button. Close the browser tab. Open SysON again, open AeroLink Week 8 Section 7 from the project list, and open view1. Click the use case, then enterprise, passengers and the objective in the Explorer.`,why:`Reopening proves that your work is in the model, not only on your screen. Check what is easiest to lose: the short names, the two typings and both Documentation entries.`,result:`All nodes and both lines are back. The use case keeps SCN-001 and its Documentation, enterprise and passengers keep Typed by, and the objective keeps its Documentation.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u29-reopened-diagram.png`,alt:`The reopened diagram with all elements.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u31-reopened-subject-enterprise-typed-by.png`,alt:`Details for enterprise after reopening, typed by AeroLink Urban Mobility.`},{src:`syson/week8/s7-u33-reopened-objective-documentation.png`,alt:`Details for the objective after reopening, with its Documentation.`}]},{title:`Inspect the Validation panel`,text:`Click the warning triangle on the left edge to open the Validation panel.`,why:`SysON’s Validation panel is separate from the OMG checker, which is the reference for whether a model is correct. Every warning on this model has been traced, and the list below this step explains each one.`,result:`The Validation panel reports 23 warnings on this correct model. Each one has been traced: none is a defect in the model, 17 are false alarms and 6 come from how SysON stores the model. The list below explains them. If your count differs, compare your model with these steps. The panel is separate from the OMG checker, which is the reference.`,warnings:s(7),imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u35-validation-panel.png`,alt:`The Validation panel with its diagnostics count.`}]},{title:`Optional: see the SysML text`,text:`Open the ⋮ menu on SysMLv2.sysml and choose Download. Open the downloaded file in any text editor and compare it with the SysML text in step 2 of this section.`,why:`You did not write this text: SysON generated it from your diagram and the fields you filled in. Nothing here needs editing.`,result:`The same definitions and use case as the listing. SysON adds a view block for its diagram and writes the objective with its default name.`,imgs:[{src:`syson/week8/s7-u34-export-document-menu-download.png`,alt:`The document menu with Download.`}]}],after:[`What you built: a use case with one subject, one actor and one objective, defined by two part definitions.`,`It is the same model as the listing in step 2, apart from the objective’s default name. When the course team exported this model with SysON’s Download command and ran the course checker on the file, it passed. SysON adds a view block for its diagram.`,`For your team’s enterprise model: use the same pattern for each of your ConOps scenarios. The enterprise is the subject, each participating stakeholder is an actor, and the success condition is the objective. If you are tempted to add a second subject, it is probably an actor.`,`Your SysON work is not marked and the app cannot see it. SysON’s export of other constructs (requirements, interfaces, viewpoints) still has known faults, so the course does not grade SysON exports.`]}},next:[{week:10,text:`Each use case gets its action flow and the logical functions that perform it.`}]},{id:`architecture`,title:`Functions, architecture and interfaces`,known:{week:4,text:`The functions (FN), the logical and physical elements (LOG, PHY) and the interfaces (IF) from Weeks 4 and 5.`},representation:`A function becomes an action. A logical element that carries out a function performs it. Physical elements are part usages with ports; an interface definition says what passes between two ports, and an interface usage connects two of them. An allocation assigns a function to a physical element.`,constructs:[{text:`action <'FN-006'> Name;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.17 Actions, pp.116–131`,cite:{reading:`R82`,pages:`116–131`}},{text:`perform 'FN-006';`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.17.6 Perform Action Usages, p.136`,cite:{reading:`R82`,pages:`136`}},{text:`port def, ~ (conjugation)`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.12 Ports, pp.93–95`,cite:{reading:`R81`,pages:`93–95`}},{text:`interface def … end …; interface … connect … to …;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.14 Interfaces, pp.106–109`,cite:{reading:`R81`,pages:`106–109`}},{text:`allocation … allocate … to …;`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.15 Allocations, pp.110–112`,cite:{reading:`R81`,pages:`110–112`}},{text:`EnergyValue`,kind:`library`,clause:`ISQ library (imported)`}],file:u(`learn_08_functions_interfaces`),focus:[{line:26,text:`The logical element LOG-002 performs the function FN-006.`},{line:32,text:`The interface IF-005 connects the charger outlet to the aircraft inlet.`},{line:35,text:`FN-006 is allocated to the charging systems PHY-003.`}],figure:{id:`interface`,kind:`native`,caption:`Interconnection view: part usages with ports drawn as small squares on their border, and the interface usage as a line between two ports. Course-simplified: the logical architecture and the allocation are not drawn.`},meaning:`Twelve aircraft (the fleet at opening) and the charging systems are parts of aeroLink; energy flows out of the charger port into the conjugated aircraft port through IF-005. The fleet-management element performs the charging function, and the function is allocated to the charging systems.`,relationships:[{text:`An interface connects two ports whose definitions match (one conjugated).`,valid:!0,why:`The ends of the interface definition fix the port types.`},{text:`perform names an action.`,valid:!0,why:`A perform action usage refers to an action.`}],readings:[{text:`~ChargingPort is a different port definition from ChargingPort.`,correct:!1,why:`It is the conjugate of the same definition: its in and out directions are reversed.`},{text:`Allocating FN-006 to PHY-003 says the charging systems are responsible for that function.`,correct:!0,why:`An allocation assigns responsibility from one element to another.`}],aerolink:[`FN-006`,`LOG-002`,`PHY-001`,`PHY-003`,`IF-005`],next:[{week:10,text:`Logical and behavioral modeling: actions, flows and performs.`},{week:11,text:`Physical modeling: ports, interfaces and allocations.`}]},{id:`views`,title:`Stakeholder views: viewpoints and views`,known:{week:4,text:`The Week 4 viewpoints: which stakeholder needs to see which part of the architecture. CON-016 is framed by VP-004 Physical.`},representation:`A viewpoint frames the concerns a stakeholder group has. A view definition satisfies a viewpoint; a view exposes the model elements to show and names a rendering from the Views library.`,constructs:[{text:`viewpoint Name { frame …; }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.26.3 Viewpoint Definitions and Usages, p.185`,cite:{reading:`R85`,pages:`185`}},{text:`view def Name { satisfy …; }   view name : Def { expose …; render …; }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.26.2 View Definitions and Usages, pp.183–185`,cite:{reading:`R85`,pages:`183–185`}},{text:`asTreeDiagram`,kind:`library`,clause:`9.2.19.2.4 asTreeDiagram (Views model library), p.542`,cite:{reading:`R125`,pages:`542`}},{text:`The diagram a tool draws from a view`,kind:`tool`,clause:`Not fixed by the specification`}],file:u(`learn_09_viewpoint_view`),focus:[{line:15,text:`The viewpoint frames the city planners’ concern.`},{line:21,text:`The view exposes the Stakeholders package and asks for a tree rendering.`}],figure:{id:`view`,kind:`tool`,caption:`What a tool might draw for this view: a tree of the exposed elements. The layout, colors and which compartments appear are the tool’s choice; the model fixes only what is exposed and which rendering is requested.`},meaning:`The siting viewpoint collects the city planning authority’s concern about land use; the stakeholder view satisfies that viewpoint by showing everything in the Stakeholders package as a tree.`,relationships:[{text:`A viewpoint frames a concern.`,valid:!0,why:`Viewpoints, like requirements, frame concerns.`},{text:`A view definition satisfies a viewpoint.`,valid:!0,why:`The view is meant to address the viewpoint’s concerns.`}],readings:[{text:`The view stores a copy of the stakeholders.`,correct:!1,why:`A view only exposes existing model elements; nothing is copied.`},{text:`Two tools may draw this view differently and both be right.`,correct:!0,why:`The rendering is named, but the drawing is tool-specific.`}],aerolink:[`ENT-001`,`STK-013`,`CON-016`],next:[{week:12,text:`Views of the digital thread for reviews.`}]},{id:`risks`,title:`Risks: library metadata`,known:{week:3,text:`The risk register (R rows): condition-if-then statement, likelihood and consequence.`},representation:`A risk becomes a metadata usage of the RiskMetadata library’s Risk definition, attached with about to the element at risk. Expressing the register’s likelihood and consequence ratings as the library’s probability and impact is a course convention.`,constructs:[{text:`metadata <'R-005'> Name : Risk about element { … }`,kind:`native`,clause:`7.27.2 Metadata Definitions and Usages, pp.191–192`,cite:{reading:`R69`,pages:`191–192`}},{text:`Risk, totalRisk, RiskLevel(probability, impact)`,kind:`library`,clause:`9.3.3 Risk Metadata, pp.558–560`,cite:{reading:`R86`,pages:`558–560`}},{text:`Expressing likelihood and consequence ratings as probability and impact`,kind:`pattern`,clause:`Course convention`}],file:u(`learn_10_risk_metadata`),focus:[{line:11,text:`R-005 is metadata about the usage chargingSystems (PHY-003), defined by the library’s Risk.`}],noFigure:`The course draws no figure here: risk metadata is read in the text or in a tool’s element table, and how a tool shows metadata is tool-specific.`,meaning:`The model records R-005 on the charging systems: the grid connection may fall below the hubs’ charging demand. Likelihood 2 of the Week 3 scale becomes probability 0.4, the top of its band, and consequence 4 becomes impact 0.8. The metadata does not change the structure of the system; it annotates it so that analyses and trade studies can find it.`,relationships:[{text:`Risk metadata about a usage.`,valid:!0,why:`about names the annotated element.`},{text:`A totalRisk value that is not a RiskLevel.`,valid:!1,why:`totalRisk is typed by the library’s RiskLevel.`}],readings:[{text:`RiskLevel is a course name.`,correct:!1,why:`It comes from the RiskMetadata library.`},{text:`Turning likelihood 2 and consequence 4 into probability 0.4 and impact 0.8 is a course choice.`,correct:!0,why:`The library defines the fields, not your rating scales.`}],aerolink:[`ENT-001`,`PHY-003`,`R-005`],next:[{week:13,text:`Trade studies weigh alternatives against risks and measures.`}]}],p=a(f),m=[{text:`Causal loop diagrams`,why:`They are Systems Thinking diagrams. Keep them as documents; cite them in a doc comment where a model element came from them.`},{text:`The interest–influence grid (Week 1)`,why:`The grid is a way to look at the ratings. The ratings themselves are metadata (section 5); the grid is not a SysML v2 view the specification defines.`},{text:`The evolution roadmap timeline (Week 6)`,why:`It is a planning chart. Its milestones may later relate to model elements, but the chart is not a model.`}];export{c as a,l as i,d as n,p as r,m as t};